window.CET4_BANKS.push({"id": "2022-09-1", "source": "2022年09月真题·第1套", "writing": {"prompt": "Directions: Suppose your university is selecting some students to teach kids in remote rural areas during the coming vacation. You are now to write an application letter to the university to explain why you want to take part and what you can do for the kids. You will have 30 minutes to write the letter. You should write at least 120 words but no more than 180 words.", "sample": "Dear Sir or Madam,\nI am writing to apply for the position of a volunteer teacher for children in remote rural areas during the\ncoming vacation. The main reason I want to take part is my genuine love for education. Two years ago, I\ntutored a left-behind child in my hometown, and watching her grow more confident convinced me that\nteaching can truly change lives. Joining this program is my way of giving back to society.\nAs for what I can do, I major in English, so I am confident that I can teach the kids basic spoken English and\nhelp them with their homework. Besides, I enjoy singing and drawing, and I would like to organize after-class\nactivities to make learning both fun and warm for them.\nI would sincerely appreciate it if I could be given this valuable chance. Thank you for your time\nand consideration.\nYours sincerely, Li Hua"}, "translation": {"prompt": "太极拳 (Taijiquan)起源于中国古代，是中国武术 (martial art) 的一个重要分支。练太极拳最初是为了自卫，现在是促进身心健康的有效锻炼方式。大量研究表明，这种锻炼方式有助于保持力量、灵活性和平衡力，并减少压力和焦虑。太极拳练习起来既容易又愉快，通过轻柔、流畅的动作，促使心情平静、头脑清晰。今天，太极拳已经传播到世界各地，深受广大健身者的喜爱。", "sample": "Taijiquan originated from ancient China and is an important branch of Chinese martial arts. Practicing Taijiquan was originally for self-defense, but it is now an effective form of exercise to promote physical and mental health. A large number of studies show that this form of exercise helps maintain strength, flexibility and balance, and reduce stress and anxiety. Taijiquan is both easy and enjoyable to practice, and with its gentle and flowing movements, it calms the mind and clears the head. Today, Taijiquan has spread to all parts of the world and is widely loved by fitness enthusiasts."}, "listeningUrl": "https://english-exam.lazynote.cn/cet4/paper/2022-09-1/?f=w", "clozePassage": "Public perception of success in the U.S. might be totally misguided.\nWhile 92% of people believe others care most about fame and  __26__  , fewer than 10% factor those qualities into their own success. This is according to the newly  __27__  study by Harvard Graduate School of Education professor Todd Smith. Smith says he was  __28__  by how past studies on success \"assumed what people will care about.\" In this study, his team \"went the  __29__  direction\" by spending years carrying out individual interviews and group surveys to see what people really talk about when they talk about success.\nAs a scientist, Smith  __30__  studied individuality for a living, and even he was surprised to find younger respondents cared more about having a  __31__  in life. Those between the ages of 18 and 34 prioritized it most, and that prioritization dropped off as respondents' ages went up. Perhaps this is because older people had fewer options when they were starting their careers, at a time when values focused more on stable incomes than  __32__  personal missions.\nOther trends included an emphasis on the importance of parenting. Being a parent  __33__  very high across the priorities of all study participants. Ultimately, Smith hopes institutions will take note of these insights  __34__  .\nHigher education institutions tend to focus on preparing students for high-paying jobs. For such institutions, from universities to workplaces, to better  __35__  people in the U.S., they'll need to understand \"what the American public highly prioritizes,\" Smith says.", "matchIntro": "How a rabbit study and an ex-student boost my hopes for a future of 'love and dignity'", "matchParagraphs": [{"label": "A", "text": "At whatever grade level teachers find themselves, from kindergarten to the final class at medical or law school, few moments stir the emotions as deeply as when former students reappear years and often decades later with an update on where their journey has taken them and what resiliencies(韧性) have been the pavement on which they've traveled."}, {"label": "B", "text": "So it was when a recent letter came from Kelli Harding, a student 21 years ago in my Peace Studies summer course in Washington. The weekly tuition-free class, in a roomy space that Ralph Nader and his Public Citizen nonprofit group provided, was discussion-based and required no useless homework or exams. Just come in and figure out how to increase peace and decrease violence. And do it today, tomorrow is too late. The course attracted mostly congressional interns(实习生), with a few exceptions like Kelli who was in Washington as an AmeriCorps volunteer."}, {"label": "C", "text": "Her year-long service included comforting AIDS patients at a free health clinic and delivering meals to the homebound. It was a world apart from her undergraduate days at the University of California-Berkeley majoring in political science. The Washington experience, which Kelli would later call \"transformative\", was the fuel that carried her into medicine to earn a master's degree in public health from Columbia University and a medical degree from the University of Rochester, and almost two decades of practice as an emergency-room psychiatrist(精神科医生) at New York-Presbyterian Hospital and a clinical professor of psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center."}, {"label": "D", "text": "Kelli's letter, a literate update on both her personal and professional life, touched my heart, and especially so when saying that two decades later she still has the course text, \"Solutions to Violence,\" and that \"it remains one of my favorite possessions.\" She lives in Lower Manhattan with her husband, Padraic, whom she met on a flight to London, and their three boys."}, {"label": "E", "text": "If Kelli stands out, it's because she is also a gifted writer. Last month, Atria Books published her book The Rabbit Effect: Live Longer, Happier, and Healthier with the Groundbreaking Science of Kindness."}, {"label": "F", "text": "With a blending of free-flowing confessional prose and scholarly research found in 461 notes, Kelli met my expectations that her ideas and ideals would be sound and singular. \"Despite our scientific progress,\" she writes, \"Americans are remarkably unhealthy. In 2016, the United States ranked forty-third in the world for life expectancy. It is also by far the world's most expensive place to get sick.\""}, {"label": "G", "text": "Enter the rabbits—not those running around in our woodlands but ones serving in two month-long medical experiments to test the effects of eating a high-fat diet and the connections between cholesterol and heart disease. With similar diets, the expectations were that all the rabbits would have similar cloggings of their arteries(动脉堵塞). Yet one group had 60% fewer of them."}, {"label": "H", "text": "The reason? Instead of receiving the standard care given to lab animals, the 60% group was watched over by a newcomer to the lab who, Kelli writes, \"handled the animals differently. When she fed her rabbits she talked to them and petted them. She didn't just pass out food, she gave them love. The studies indicate something is missing in the traditional biomedical model. It wasn't diet or genetics that made a difference in which rabbits got sick and which stayed healthy. It was kindness.\""}, {"label": "I", "text": "Amid the political noise about Obamacare, Medicare, Medicaid, health insurance and thieving pharmaceutical(医药的) companies, Kelli Harding stands apart from the crowd calling for quick fixes, the simpler the better. She has walked too many miles in the halls of hospitals visiting too many far-gone patients and seeing too many medical mistakes to go along with conventional thinking."}, {"label": "J", "text": "\"The rabbit effect,\" she explains, means that \"when it comes to our health, we've been missing some crucial pieces: hidden factors behind what really makes us healthy. Factors like love, friendship, and dignity. The designs of our neighborhoods, schools, and workplaces. There's a social dimension to health that we've completely overlooked in our efforts to find the best and most cutting-edge medical care … Ultimately, what affects our health in the most meaningful ways has more to do with how we treat one another, how we live, and how we think about what it means to be human than with anything that happens in the doctor's office.\""}, {"label": "K", "text": "In more than a few passages, she relates the stories of men and women who came up against assembly-line medicine where patients were treated mostly as pieces of flesh. \"Clinically,\" she writes, \"it's common to see two patients with the same condition, such as recovering from a heart attack, have two very different courses based on seemingly irrelevant factors, such as their family relationships or their educational levels. In my practice, the sickest people I see often share similar backgrounds: loneliness, abuse, poverty, or discrimination. For them, the medical model isn't enough. It's like fixing up an airplane engine and ignoring that the pilot is on his third drink at the bar and a massive storm is overhead … To properly care for patients, we also need to care about the lives of the people getting the care.\""}, {"label": "L", "text": "Kelli wastes no time taking potshots at(随意批评) the medical establishment and its body-centered biomedicine methods. Instead, she remains positive, holding up for praise one of her medical school professors, George Engel, \"who always noticed not just a patient's physical condition but little details about her life, such as if she had family pictures up in her hospital room or flowers delivered. He was the kind of trusted doctor you'd feel relieved to see and welcome into the room with a sick family member. He'd sit down to talk with the patient not just about medical problems, but about her life and priorities. He built a large consultation service to address the holistic(整体的) needs of hospitalized patients, including psychological and social factors.\""}, {"label": "M", "text": "It's a guess how many George Engels in their white jackets are at work these days and another speculation on the number of Kelli Hardings the nation is blessed with. May the totals be large and getting larger."}], "readingPassages": {"46": "Academic dishonesty is nothing new. As long as there have been homework assignments and tests, there have been cheaters. The way that cheating looks has changed over time, though, particularly now that technology has made it easier than ever. A study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics interviewed 23,000 high school students and asked them a variety of questions about academic ethics. Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an exam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior. A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via smartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had ever cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nIn today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on the insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their classmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their cheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes. Since cheating through technology is not listed specifically as being against the rules in many school policies, students do not view the actions as unethical.\nThe technology is being adopted so quickly that school districts cannot adequately keep up with cheating policies, or even awareness campaigns that alert students to the problem with using technology to find answers in a certain way. From a young age, students learn that answers exist conveniently at their fingertips through search engines and expert websites.\nSchools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must be updated consistently. Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and how technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process. Parents must also talk to their kids about the appropriate ways to find academic answers and alert them to unethical behaviours that may seem innocent in their own eyes.", "47": "Academic dishonesty is nothing new. As long as there have been homework assignments and tests, there have been cheaters. The way that cheating looks has changed over time, though, particularly now that technology has made it easier than ever. A study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics interviewed 23,000 high school students and asked them a variety of questions about academic ethics. Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an exam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior. A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via smartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had ever cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nIn today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on the insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their classmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their cheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes. Since cheating through technology is not listed specifically as being against the rules in many school policies, students do not view the actions as unethical.\nThe technology is being adopted so quickly that school districts cannot adequately keep up with cheating policies, or even awareness campaigns that alert students to the problem with using technology to find answers in a certain way. From a young age, students learn that answers exist conveniently at their fingertips through search engines and expert websites.\nSchools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must be updated consistently. Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and how technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process. Parents must also talk to their kids about the appropriate ways to find academic answers and alert them to unethical behaviours that may seem innocent in their own eyes.", "48": "Academic dishonesty is nothing new. As long as there have been homework assignments and tests, there have been cheaters. The way that cheating looks has changed over time, though, particularly now that technology has made it easier than ever. A study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics interviewed 23,000 high school students and asked them a variety of questions about academic ethics. Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an exam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior. A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via smartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had ever cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nIn today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on the insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their classmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their cheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes. Since cheating through technology is not listed specifically as being against the rules in many school policies, students do not view the actions as unethical.\nThe technology is being adopted so quickly that school districts cannot adequately keep up with cheating policies, or even awareness campaigns that alert students to the problem with using technology to find answers in a certain way. From a young age, students learn that answers exist conveniently at their fingertips through search engines and expert websites.\nSchools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must be updated consistently. Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and how technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process. Parents must also talk to their kids about the appropriate ways to find academic answers and alert them to unethical behaviours that may seem innocent in their own eyes.", "49": "Academic dishonesty is nothing new. As long as there have been homework assignments and tests, there have been cheaters. The way that cheating looks has changed over time, though, particularly now that technology has made it easier than ever. A study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics interviewed 23,000 high school students and asked them a variety of questions about academic ethics. Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an exam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior. A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via smartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had ever cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nIn today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on the insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their classmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their cheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes. Since cheating through technology is not listed specifically as being against the rules in many school policies, students do not view the actions as unethical.\nThe technology is being adopted so quickly that school districts cannot adequately keep up with cheating policies, or even awareness campaigns that alert students to the problem with using technology to find answers in a certain way. From a young age, students learn that answers exist conveniently at their fingertips through search engines and expert websites.\nSchools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must be updated consistently. Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and how technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process. Parents must also talk to their kids about the appropriate ways to find academic answers and alert them to unethical behaviours that may seem innocent in their own eyes.", "50": "Academic dishonesty is nothing new. As long as there have been homework assignments and tests, there have been cheaters. The way that cheating looks has changed over time, though, particularly now that technology has made it easier than ever. A study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics interviewed 23,000 high school students and asked them a variety of questions about academic ethics. Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an exam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior. A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via smartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had ever cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nIn today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on the insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their classmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their cheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes. Since cheating through technology is not listed specifically as being against the rules in many school policies, students do not view the actions as unethical.\nThe technology is being adopted so quickly that school districts cannot adequately keep up with cheating policies, or even awareness campaigns that alert students to the problem with using technology to find answers in a certain way. From a young age, students learn that answers exist conveniently at their fingertips through search engines and expert websites.\nSchools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must be updated consistently. Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and how technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process. Parents must also talk to their kids about the appropriate ways to find academic answers and alert them to unethical behaviours that may seem innocent in their own eyes.", "51": "Remote work is about more than just working from home—it means working differently. Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures in light of the shift to remote work. This requires a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from employees and what companies would look for in a model employee in a remote work context.\nIt is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should be replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization. This means that work should be measured in terms of the quality of the work, not just the quality of the process. As remote work is largely unobservable to supervisors, employers need to think about how they can objectively measure the quality of work in a way that is consistent for employees of similar rank.\nFocusing on output alone can have unintended consequences. Employers should think instead about the values and soft skills they want to emphasize in a remote work environment. Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might become critical.\nMuch has been written about the importance of timely feedback. In the context of a global pandemic(大流行病), firms may want to provide additional support to employees by providing more frequent communication. This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\nCompensation also needs to be revisited. The purpose of performance evaluation is ultimately to determine how to reward employees for their work. This means that pay structures need to adapt to the reality of working from home. However, organizations also need to be honest with employees about the financial impact of COVID-19.\nFor organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might mean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible work schedules. Employers can also consider how to bundle different types of compensation to help employees cope with their unique situations.", "52": "Remote work is about more than just working from home—it means working differently. Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures in light of the shift to remote work. This requires a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from employees and what companies would look for in a model employee in a remote work context.\nIt is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should be replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization. This means that work should be measured in terms of the quality of the work, not just the quality of the process. As remote work is largely unobservable to supervisors, employers need to think about how they can objectively measure the quality of work in a way that is consistent for employees of similar rank.\nFocusing on output alone can have unintended consequences. Employers should think instead about the values and soft skills they want to emphasize in a remote work environment. Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might become critical.\nMuch has been written about the importance of timely feedback. In the context of a global pandemic(大流行病), firms may want to provide additional support to employees by providing more frequent communication. This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\nCompensation also needs to be revisited. The purpose of performance evaluation is ultimately to determine how to reward employees for their work. This means that pay structures need to adapt to the reality of working from home. However, organizations also need to be honest with employees about the financial impact of COVID-19.\nFor organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might mean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible work schedules. Employers can also consider how to bundle different types of compensation to help employees cope with their unique situations.", "53": "Remote work is about more than just working from home—it means working differently. Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures in light of the shift to remote work. This requires a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from employees and what companies would look for in a model employee in a remote work context.\nIt is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should be replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization. This means that work should be measured in terms of the quality of the work, not just the quality of the process. As remote work is largely unobservable to supervisors, employers need to think about how they can objectively measure the quality of work in a way that is consistent for employees of similar rank.\nFocusing on output alone can have unintended consequences. Employers should think instead about the values and soft skills they want to emphasize in a remote work environment. Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might become critical.\nMuch has been written about the importance of timely feedback. In the context of a global pandemic(大流行病), firms may want to provide additional support to employees by providing more frequent communication. This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\nCompensation also needs to be revisited. The purpose of performance evaluation is ultimately to determine how to reward employees for their work. This means that pay structures need to adapt to the reality of working from home. However, organizations also need to be honest with employees about the financial impact of COVID-19.\nFor organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might mean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible work schedules. Employers can also consider how to bundle different types of compensation to help employees cope with their unique situations.", "54": "Remote work is about more than just working from home—it means working differently. Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures in light of the shift to remote work. This requires a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from employees and what companies would look for in a model employee in a remote work context.\nIt is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should be replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization. This means that work should be measured in terms of the quality of the work, not just the quality of the process. As remote work is largely unobservable to supervisors, employers need to think about how they can objectively measure the quality of work in a way that is consistent for employees of similar rank.\nFocusing on output alone can have unintended consequences. Employers should think instead about the values and soft skills they want to emphasize in a remote work environment. Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might become critical.\nMuch has been written about the importance of timely feedback. In the context of a global pandemic(大流行病), firms may want to provide additional support to employees by providing more frequent communication. This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\nCompensation also needs to be revisited. The purpose of performance evaluation is ultimately to determine how to reward employees for their work. This means that pay structures need to adapt to the reality of working from home. However, organizations also need to be honest with employees about the financial impact of COVID-19.\nFor organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might mean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible work schedules. Employers can also consider how to bundle different types of compensation to help employees cope with their unique situations.", "55": "Remote work is about more than just working from home—it means working differently. Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures in light of the shift to remote work. This requires a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from employees and what companies would look for in a model employee in a remote work context.\nIt is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should be replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization. This means that work should be measured in terms of the quality of the work, not just the quality of the process. As remote work is largely unobservable to supervisors, employers need to think about how they can objectively measure the quality of work in a way that is consistent for employees of similar rank.\nFocusing on output alone can have unintended consequences. Employers should think instead about the values and soft skills they want to emphasize in a remote work environment. Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might become critical.\nMuch has been written about the importance of timely feedback. In the context of a global pandemic(大流行病), firms may want to provide additional support to employees by providing more frequent communication. This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\nCompensation also needs to be revisited. The purpose of performance evaluation is ultimately to determine how to reward employees for their work. This means that pay structures need to adapt to the reality of working from home. However, organizations also need to be honest with employees about the financial impact of COVID-19.\nFor organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might mean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible work schedules. Employers can also consider how to bundle different types of compensation to help employees cope with their unique situations."}, "questions": [{"id": "2022-09-1-l-1", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 1, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the study?", "options": ["A) It examines the effect of cholesterol on people's health.", "B) Its participants all had high blood cholesterol levels.", "C) It questions the benefits of a vegetarian protein diet.", "D) Its finding came as a surprise to the researchers."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Its finding came as a surprise to the researchers.\n【定位】This finding surprised researchers, who admitted they didn't expect that eating white meat\nwould lead to higher blood cholesterol levels.\n这一发现令研究者们感到意外,他们承认自己没有料到吃白肉会导致血液胆固醇水平升高。\n【信号】情绪信号词 surprised。答案落点不是平铺的数据句,而是带情绪色彩的 This finding\nsurprised researchers,再用 who admitted they didn't expect 加重——新闻里出现\"意\n外/没料到\"这种态度词,往往就是出题点。\n【替换】This finding surprised researchers ↔ Its finding came as a surprise to the researchers\n动词 surprised 改写成名词 a surprise\nwho admitted they didn't expect ↔ came as a surprise\n\"没料到\"与\"意外\"同义互证\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\nA 借了原文反复出现的 blood cholesterol levels 里的 cholesterol 一词,把研究说成\"考察\n胆固醇对健康的影响\";但录音考察的是\"不同肉类的饮食对胆固醇的影响\",胆固醇是被测的结\n果而非被研究的起因,A 把对象说反了。\n借用原文：blood cholesterol levels\nB) 与原文相反\nB 说参与者\"都是高胆固醇\",但第二段原文是 researchers looked at 113 healthy\npeople(113 名健康人);B 盗用了 cholesterol levels 这个反复出现的词,再加个绝对的 all,\n把\"健康人\"悄悄换成\"高胆固醇人群\",与原文相反。\n借用原文：researchers looked at 113 healthy people\nC) 原词陷阱\nC 说研究\"质疑素食蛋白饮食的好处\",借的是原文真出现过的 a vegetarian protein diet;但\n原文第四段恰恰是 eat...more vegetarian protein(建议多吃素食蛋白),研究从头到尾力挺素\n食蛋白、绝无质疑,属无中生有又反向。\n借用原文：a vegetarian protein diet", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-2", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 2, "stem": "What did the researchers acknowledge?", "options": ["A) They do not know all the effects of eating meat.", "B) Red meat itself does not cause heart diseases.", "C) White meat may be healthier than red meat.", "D) Vegetarian protein may be easier to absorb."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） White meat may be healthier than red meat.\n【定位】The team acknowledged that it is possible that white meat is better for our health than red\nmeat despite their findings.\n研究团队承认,尽管有这些发现,白肉仍有可能比红肉更有益于我们的健康。\n【信号】题干同词 acknowledged 引出让步。答案句正是题干 acknowledge 在原文里的对应词\nThe team acknowledged that it is possible that,其后 white meat is better for our\nhealth than red meat 就是承认的内容;\"题干动词在原文复现\"是最稳的定位信号。\n【替换】white meat is better for our health than red meat ↔ White meat may be healthier than red meat\nbetter for our health 改写成 healthier\nit is possible that ↔ may be\nit is possible that 等于 may\n【排除】A) 过度推断\nA 借了第四段 other effects of red meat consumption 里的 effects,说研究者\"承认还不了\n解吃肉的全部影响\";但原文只说红肉\"另有别的影响可能致病\",并没说研究者\"不知道全部影\n响\",A 把局部说法夸大成笼统的\"全不知\",属过度推断。\n借用原文：other effects of red meat consumption\nB) 与原文相反\nB 借了原文 contribute to heart disease 里的 heart disease,说\"红肉本身不致心脏病\";但\n原文恰说红肉的别的影响\"可能促成心脏病\",方向正相反,B 把原文的肯定关系反成了否定。\n借用原文：contribute to heart disease\nD) 未提及\nD 借了原文反复出现的 vegetarian protein,却安上一个原文压根没提的角度 absorb(更易吸\n收);全文只说建议多吃素食蛋白、它对胆固醇影响更小,从未谈\"吸收\",属无中生有。\n借用原文：vegetarian protein\nQuestions 3 and 4 are based on the news report you have just heard.\n1 At around half past nine this morning, a trailer attached to a lorry turned over at the crossing of High\nStreet in Milton. Hundreds of frozen turkeys were spilled all over the road. It is reported that nobody\nwas hurt in the incident, but police said it may affect traffic and Christmas dinners. With just one week\nto go before Christmas, there are worries that local supermarket supplies of this holiday favorite may\nbe affected.\n今天上午九点半左右,一辆卡车拖挂的拖车在Milton的High Street十字路口发生侧翻。数百只冷冻火鸡散\n落在路面上。据报道,此次事故中无人受伤,但警方表示这可能会影响交通和圣诞晚餐。距离圣诞节仅剩一\n周时间,人们担心当地超市这一节日热门食品的供应可能会受到影响。\n2 A police spokeswoman said that officers were currently in attendance at the scene. She stated that the\ndriver of the lorry had been arrested on suspicion of dangerous driving.\n一名警方女发言人表示,警员目前正在现场处置。她说,卡车司机已因涉嫌危险驾驶被逮捕。\n3 The crossing on High Street is a well-known accident blackspot. This year alone, there have been\nseven traffic accidents at this location. Thankfully, none of these accidents have resulted in serious injury.\nHigh Street的这个十字路口是一个众所周知的交通事故多发点。仅今年一年,该路口就发生了七起交通事\n故。幸运的是,这些事故均未造成重伤。", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-3", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 3, "stem": "What does the news report say about the accident at the crossing of High Street in Milton?", "options": ["A) It may have been due to the lorry driver's drunk driving.", "B) It may affect the local supply of turkeys for Christmas.", "C) It interrupted traffic for several hours running.", "D) It was caused by a lorry running into a trailer."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It may affect the local supply of turkeys for Christmas.\n【定位】It is reported that nobody was hurt in the incident, but police said it may affect traffic and\nChristmas dinners. With just one week to go before Christmas, there are worries that local\nsupermarket supplies of this holiday favorite may be affected.\n据报道，事故中无人受伤，但警方表示这可能影响交通和圣诞晚餐。距圣诞节仅剩一周，人\n们担心当地超市这一节日抢手货的供应可能受影响。\n【信号】转折 but 后接真正落点。答案藏在转折词后面：nobody was hurt in the incident, but\npolice said it may affect traffic and Christmas dinners 里 but 之后才是报道强调的影\n响，紧接着 supplies of this holiday favorite may be affected 进一步点明\"节日抢手\n货\"（即火鸡）供应受影响，这正是答案句。\n【替换】supplies of this holiday favorite may be affected ↔\nIt may affect the local supply of turkeys for Christmas.\nthis holiday favorite=turkeys\nmay be affected ↔ It may affect\n被动转主动·affect 同词根\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n录音说司机 had been arrested on suspicion of dangerous driving（涉嫌危险驾驶被\n捕），选项却写成 drunk driving（酒驾）。dangerous driving 与 drunk driving 长得\n像、都沾\"driving\"，但危险驾驶不等于酒驾，原文从没提到喝酒，属偷换。\n借用原文：the driver of the lorry had been arrested on suspicion of dangerous driving\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 interrupted traffic for several hours running 里\"中断交通\"沾了原文 it may affect\ntraffic 的边——traffic 确实被念到，所以听到就想选；但原文只说\"可能影响交通\"，从没\n说\"中断了好几个小时\"，several hours 是凭空加的时长。\n借用原文：it may affect traffic and Christmas dinners\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 It was caused by a lorry running into a trailer 把 lorry、trailer 两个原词都用上\n了，极像答案；但原文是 a trailer attached to a lorry turned over——挂在卡车上的拖车\n翻倒，并不是\"一辆卡车撞上一辆拖车\"，选项把\"翻车\"歪曲成了\"撞车\"，动作对不上。\n借用原文：a trailer attached to a lorry turned over at the crossing of High Street", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-4", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 4, "stem": "What do we learn about the crossing on High Street?", "options": ["A) It has been the scene of several fatal accidents recently.", "B) It is the spot that causes the local police a lot of worry.", "C) It has witnessed several traffic accidents this year.", "D) It is a location frequented by local traffic police."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It has witnessed several traffic accidents this year.\n【定位】The crossing on High Street is a well-known accident blackspot. This year alone, there have\nbeen seven traffic accidents at this location. Thankfully, none of these accidents have resulted\nin serious injury.\n高街的这个路口是出了名的事故黑点。仅今年一年，这个地点就发生了七起交通事故。所幸\n这些事故没有一起造成严重伤亡。\n【信号】数字+时间范围·具体数据句。答案落在带具体数据的那句：This year alone, there have\nbeen seven traffic accidents at this location——\"今年\"这个时间范围加上\"七起\"这个数\n字，正是新闻里最常被设题的信息点，听到数字就该锁定。\n【替换】there have been seven traffic accidents ↔ It has witnessed several traffic accidents\nseven=several·具体数概括成\"几起\"\nThis year alone ↔ this year\n时间范围一致·this year\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n选项 several fatal accidents 借用了原文 accident 一词，但 fatal（致命的，会死人）与原\n文恰好相反——录音明说 none of these accidents have resulted in serious injury（没\n有一起造成严重伤亡），更别提致命，方向反了。\n借用原文：none of these accidents have resulted in serious injury\nB) 未提及\n选项 causes the local police a lot of worry 用了 police 这个原文出现过的词（A police\nspokeswoman、officers were currently in attendance）来诱选，但原文只说警方到场处\n理、发言人通报，从没说这个路口\"让警方很担忧\"，担忧是凭空加的。\n借用原文：officers were currently in attendance at the scene\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 frequented by local traffic police 把 traffic（原文 it may affect traffic 念过）和\npolice（A police spokeswoman 念过）拼在一起，听着字字耳熟；但原文从没说\"交警经\n常光顾这个路口\"，frequented 这层意思是编出来的。\n借用原文：A police spokeswoman said that officers were currently in attendance at the scene", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-5", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 5, "stem": "What is Bangalore airport trying to do about the helicopter taxi service?", "options": ["A) Get approval to add more routes.", "B) Attract more international tourists.", "C) Advertise it through a mobile app.", "D) Make it affordable to common folk."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Get approval to add more routes.\n【定位】The service, which claims to be the first of its kind in India, offers only one route, but\nBangalore airport will add more once it gets approval.\n这项服务号称是印度同类中的首创，目前只提供一条线路，但班加罗尔机场一旦获批就会增\n加更多线路。\n【信号】but 后\\+将来动作。答案句靠两个信号锁定：一是转折词 but，新闻里 but 之后常落真正\n要说的事；二是将来动作 will add more once it gets approval，正好对应题干\"trying to\ndo（正打算做的事）\"。\n【替换】will add more once it gets approval ↔ Get approval to add more routes\n同一动作换序表达\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n选项 Attract more international tourists. 借\"国际\"二字勾连录音里的 International\nAirport terminal（国际机场航站楼），但 International 在原文只是机场名字的一部分，录\n音从未说机场要\"吸引国际游客\"，属听到 International 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：International Airport terminal\nC) 原词陷阱\n选项 Advertise it through a mobile app. 盗用录音 Customers can book their\nhelicopter ride through a mobile app 里的 mobile app，但原文说 app 是用来\"预订\n（book）\"的，不是机场用来\"打广告（advertise）\"，动作被偷换，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：Customers can book their helicopter ride through a mobile app\nD) 与原文相反\n选项 Make it affordable to common folk. 搬来录音里的 affordable，但原文是\nHelicopter taxi is not an affordable option for many travelers（对许多人并不便宜），\n机场也明说 This is not a low cost option，根本没打算降价惠民，选项把\"不便宜\"反着说\n成\"要让普通人买得起\"，是与原文相反的陷阱。\n借用原文：Helicopter taxi is not an affordable option for many travelers", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-6", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 6, "stem": "What do we learn from the news report about the helicopter taxi ride?", "options": ["A) It costs more than twice as much as a car ride.", "B) It is gaining popularity among ordinary Indians.", "C) It symbolizes India's advancement in high-tech.", "D) It can get anywhere in the city within 15 minutes."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It costs more than twice as much as a car ride.\n【定位】Helicopter taxi is not an affordable option for many travelers. A car ride for the same journey\ncosts less than half as much.\n直升机出租车对许多旅客来说并不便宜。同样的行程，打车的花费还不到它的一半。\n【信号】价格比较句。答案句是带比较结构的事实陈述 A car ride for the same journey costs less\nthan half as much，含 less than half 这种倍数/比例信号，正对应选项里的价格数字题，\n是听力里\"听到数字/比较就锁定\"的典型答案句。\n【替换】A car ride for the same journey costs less than half as much ↔\nIt costs more than twice as much as a car ride\n比例反向换算\n【排除】B) 与原文相反\n选项 It is gaining popularity among ordinary Indians. 与录音 not an affordable\noption for many travelers（对许多人并不便宜）相悖——既然普通人嫌贵，就谈不上\n在\"普通印度人\"中流行；选项借\"印度\"这个大背景词制造熟悉感，实则与原文相反。\n借用原文：not an affordable option for many travelers\nC) 过度推断\n选项 It symbolizes India’s advancement in high-tech. 把这项服务上升到\"象征印度高科\n技进步\"，但录音通篇只讲它的线路、价格、客户、运营方，从未做这种象征性评价，属录\n音根本没说的过度拔高。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 It can get anywhere in the city within 15 minutes. 盗用录音的 15 分钟，但原文\nferry customers the 40 miles between Bangalore’s Electronic City tech hub and the\nInternational Airport terminal in 15 minutes 说的是\"特定两点间 40 英里 15 分钟\"，选\n项偷偷把范围放大成\"城内任何地方\"，数字对、范围被改大，属原词陷阱。\n借用原文：between Bangalore’s Electronic City tech hub and the International Airport terminal in\n15 minutes", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-7", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 7, "stem": "Who are the targeted customers of the helicopter taxi service?", "options": ["A) International tourists.", "B) High-class travelers.", "C) Prominent superstars.", "D) Customers in a hurry."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） High-class travelers.\n【定位】\"A large number of high-class travelers, including CEOs, have to spend more than three hours\nby road to get there and that is a loss of time,\" a Bangalore airport spokesperson said.\n\"包括首席执行官在内的大量高端旅客，开车去那里要花三个多小时，这是时间上的损\n失，\"班加罗尔机场一位发言人说。\n【信号】发言人点名人群。答案句是发言人原话里直接点名的人群 A large number of high-class\ntravelers, including CEOs，新闻里\"官方发言人/负责人\"的引语常承载关键信息，且这里直\n接报出目标客户，是最可靠的答案信号。\n【替换】A large number of high-class travelers, including CEOs ↔ High-class travelers\n原话即选项措辞\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n选项 International tourists. 借 International 勾连录音里多次出现的 International\nAirport terminal，但原文从没说目标客户是\"国际游客\"，International 只属于机场名字，\n属听到原词就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：International Airport terminal\nC) 未提及\n选项 Prominent superstars.（知名巨星）在录音里根本没出现，发言人点的是 high-class\ntravelers 和 CEOs，从未提明星；这是\"凭直觉觉得贵东西该卖给名人\"的常识陷阱，原文无\n据。\nD) 原词陷阱\n选项 Customers in a hurry. 盗用录音 tech executives in a hurry 里的 in a hurry，但原\n文 in a hurry 只是修饰\"赶时间的科技高管\"，回答\"为谁服务\"的官方表述用的是 high-class\ntravelers 这个上位称呼；选项抓住次要修饰语 in a hurry 当答案，偷换了概念层级，属原\n词陷阱。\n借用原文：tech executives in a hurry", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-8", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 8, "stem": "What does the woman plan to do for the weekend?", "options": ["A) Treat her friends in a bar.", "B) Take a trip to Washington.", "C) Make some cheese.", "D) Throw a party."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Throw a party.\n【定位】It will be very casual. We will just be a small group of friends gathering together at\nWashington Park. There will be around 20 of us, including children.\n会很随意。我们就是一小群朋友聚在华盛顿公园，大约20个人，还带着孩子。\n【信号】女方自述聚会安排。答案藏在女方对自己活动的描述里：a small group of friends\ngathering together 加上前文 a barbecue this weekend，合起来说明她要办一场朋友聚\n会/派对，这是问\"她计划做什么\"时该锁定的自述句。\n【替换】a small group of friends gathering together ↔ Throw a party.\n聚会即办派对\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n原文确实有 friends，但聚会在 Washington Park 公园而非酒吧，bar 是凭空加的；选项\n借\"朋友\"这个原词诱你选，地点对不上。\n借用原文：a small group of friends gathering together at Washington Park\nB) 原词陷阱\n原文 Washington Park 只是聚会地点，选项把 Washington 拎出来说成 take a trip，\n把\"在华盛顿公园聚会\"歪曲成\"去华盛顿旅行\"。\n借用原文：gathering together at Washington Park\nC) 原词陷阱\n全程女方是去 buy some cheese，从没说要\"做\"奶酪；选项借高频词 cheese 诱选，把\nbuy 偷换成 make。\n借用原文：buy some cheese for a barbecue", "points": ["听力·长对话", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-9", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 9, "stem": "What does the man suggest the woman do?", "options": ["A) Spend no more than 30 dollars.", "B) Buy different kinds of cheese.", "C) Help him prepare the barbecue.", "D) Find out different people's tastes."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Buy different kinds of cheese.\n【定位】I would suggest having at least one soft cheese and one hard cheese. That will offer you a\ngood variety to suit different people's tastes.\n我建议至少买一种软奶酪和一种硬奶酪。这样能提供丰富的选择，照顾到不同人的口味。\n【信号】建议信号词 suggest。I would suggest having at least one soft cheese and one hard\ncheese 里的 suggest 是明确的建议信号，紧随其后的内容就是男方给的建议，正是答案\n落点。\n【替换】one soft cheese and one hard cheese ↔ Buy different kinds of cheese.\n软硬各一=多种\na good variety to suit different people's tastes ↔ Buy different kinds of cheese.\n丰富选择即多种\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n30 dollars 出自女方 Let's say $30.，是她自报的预算，不是男方的建议；选项盗用这个数\n字，又把它说成\"花不超过30\"，与\"男方建议\"无关。\n借用原文：Let's say $30.\nC) 原词陷阱\nbarbecue 在 a barbecue this weekend 出现过，但男方从未建议女方\"帮忙准备烧烤\"，\nhelp prepare 是凭空添加。\n借用原文：a barbecue this weekend\nD) 原词陷阱\ndifferent people's tastes 确在原文 to suit different people's tastes，但它是\"买软硬两\n种\"想达到的目的，不是男方让她\"去了解口味\"这个动作；选项盗用原词却答非所问。\n借用原文：to suit different people's tastes", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-10", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 10, "stem": "What does the man say about Spanish goat cheese?", "options": ["A) It is the best kind of hard cheese.", "B) It is the most popular in Spain.", "C) It is more delicious than honey.", "D) It is a good choice for children."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） It is a good choice for children.\n【定位】I would suggest something like this Spanish goat cheese. It's only $15, a very good price. You\ncan spread it on bread with a knife, and then add a tiny bit of honey on top. It's delicious.\nChildren love it.\n我建议这种西班牙山羊奶酪。只要15美元，价格很划算。你可以用刀把它抹在面包上，再在\n上面加一点蜂蜜，很美味，孩子们都爱吃。\n【信号】落点在 Children love it。讲西班牙山羊奶酪的几句里，结尾 Children love it. 是最贴近选\n项的评价句，这句直接给出\"孩子爱吃\"这一可与选项对应的信息。\n【替换】Children love it. ↔ It is a good choice for children.\n孩子爱吃=适合孩子\n【排除】A) 偷换概念\n西班牙山羊奶酪在原文是作为软奶酪 soft cheeses are creamy 举例的，根本不是 hard\ncheese，更没说\"最好的硬奶酪\"；best 也是凭空拔高。\n借用原文：soft cheeses are creamy and go well with sweet things\nB) 原词陷阱\nSpanish 确实出现在 Spanish goat cheese，但原文只说它产自西班牙/价格好，从没说\n它\"在西班牙最受欢迎\"；most popular 是无中生有。\n借用原文：this Spanish goat cheese\nC) 原词陷阱\nhoney 出自 add a tiny bit of honey on top，原文是说\"加点蜂蜜更美味\"，并没拿奶酪和\n蜂蜜比谁更好吃；选项把\"配蜂蜜\"歪成\"比蜂蜜好吃\"。\n借用原文：add a tiny bit of honey on top. It's delicious.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-11", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 11, "stem": "What is the woman going to do at the end of the conversation?", "options": ["A) Buy what the man recommended.", "B) Have a taste of both of the cheeses.", "C) Choose one of the two types of cheese.", "D) Ask the man to cut the cheese into slices."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Buy what the man recommended.\n【定位】Okay, I'll take both. Thank you for your help.\n好的，两种我都要。谢谢你的帮助。\n【信号】结尾表态 I'll take both。对话末句 I'll take both. 是女方的最终拍板，I'll 是结尾决定的信\n号，这句决定了她要怎么做。\n【替换】I'll take both. ↔ Buy what the man recommended.\n都要=买他推荐的\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n女方说的是 I'll take both.（两种都买下），不是\"先尝两种\"；选项盗用\"两种\"这个概念却把\ntake（买）偷换成 taste（尝）。\n借用原文：I'll take both.\nC) 与原文相反\n原文 I'll take both. 明说\"都要\"，选项却说\"二选一\"，方向正好相反；two types 这个原词\n对得上，结论却反了。\n借用原文：I'll take both.\nD) 张冠李戴\ncut...into slices 出自男方介绍硬奶酪 You can cut it into thin slices，是吃法说明，女方结\n尾从没让对方切片；选项把男方的话安到女方动作上。\n借用原文：You can cut it into thin slices and eat it on its own.", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-12", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 12, "stem": "What does the woman say about using e-textbooks?", "options": ["A) New teachers and staff have to be recruited.", "B) It might take some time for students to adapt.", "C) It involves buying lots of tablets and software.", "D) The software has to be constantly upgraded."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It involves buying lots of tablets and software.\n【定位】What about the cost, not only buying all those tablets, but the software and electronic\ninfrastructure that goes with it, not to mention retraining all the teachers and\nadministration staff?\n那成本怎么办呢？不光是买那些平板，还有随之而来的软件和电子基础设施，更别提还要重\n新培训所有教师和行政人员了。\n【信号】女士用反问抛出顾虑。男士刚说期待换电子书，女士立刻用 Really? What about the cost\n反问抛出\"成本\"这一顾虑——对话里一方的质疑/反问句往往就是该方观点的答案句，这句\n把\"买平板和软件\"列在最前。\n【替换】buying all those tablets, but the software ↔ buying lots of tablets and software\n原话\"买平板和软件\"=选项\"买大量平板和软件\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说的是 retraining all the teachers and administration staff（重新培训现有教师和行\n政人员），A 把它偷换成 New teachers and staff have to be recruited.（招聘新教师和\n员工）。\"培训\"不等于\"招新人\"，听到 staff 一词就选 A 正中陷阱。\n借用原文：retraining all the teachers and administration staff\nB) 未提及\nIt might take some time for students to adapt. 说学生适应需要时间，但女士的顾虑全\n在成本和环境上，整段对话没有任何一句谈\"学生适应电子书要花时间\"，属凭常识脑补的未\n提及项。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe software has to be constantly upgraded. 的\"软件升级\"出自男士 the software for\nelectronic readers can be updated instantly，而且男士是把它当\"好处\"说的；本题问女\n士的顾虑，把男士的话张冠李戴到女士头上、又把好处当代价，双重错位。\n借用原文：the software for electronic readers can be updated instantly", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-13", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 13, "stem": "According to the man, how can the use of tablets benefit students?", "options": ["A) It can greatly improve their learning efficiency.", "B) It can help them to interact more with teachers.", "C) It can save their trouble of carrying printed books.", "D) It can develop their skills in using electronic devices."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It can greatly improve their learning efficiency.\n【定位】Anyway, tablets help students learn up to 80% faster.\n总之，平板能帮学生的学习速度提升多达 80%。\n【信号】男士用 Anyway 收束抛出核心好处。男士先回应成本问题，再用 Anyway, tablets help\nstudents learn up to 80% faster 把话题收回到\"平板的好处\"上——Anyway 后的总结句配\n上 80% 这个具体数字，正是他强调的核心益处，是答案句。\n【替换】tablets help students learn up to 80% faster ↔ greatly improve their learning efficiency\n\"学得快 80%\"=\"大幅提升学习效率\"\n【排除】B) 未提及\nIt can help them to interact more with teachers. 说能多和老师互动，但男士讲的好处\n是\"学得快\"和能高亮、记笔记、快速搜索，没有一句提到\"与老师互动更多\"，是凭\"电子设备\n促进互动\"的印象脑补出来的。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音多次出现 printed textbooks/printed books，C 借这个原词编出 It can save their\ntrouble of carrying printed books.（省去背纸质书的麻烦）。但男士说的好处是高亮、记\n笔记、搜索快，从没说\"省得背书\"，是听到 printed books 就想选的原词陷阱。\n借用原文：printed textbooks\nD) 偷换概念\n录音谈的是 tablets contain many technological features（平板有很多技术功能），D 把\n它偷换成 It can develop their skills in using electronic devices.（培养学生用电子设备的\n技能）。男士讲的是功能带来的便利，不是\"练会用设备\"，借 technological/electronic 这\n层词制造混淆。\n借用原文：tablets contain many technological features", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-14", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 14, "stem": "What does the woman say about students using tablets?", "options": ["A) They may have trouble comprehending texts.", "B) They may encounter technological problems.", "C) They may pay less respect to teachers.", "D) They may get distracted more easily."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They may get distracted more easily.\n【定位】Not necessarily. Tablets have too many distractions. Students may pay attention to apps,\ngames and websites instead of their teachers.\n未必。平板有太多让人分心的东西。学生可能会去关注各种应用、游戏和网站，而不是听老\n师讲课。\n【信号】Not necessarily 否定后才是女士真意。女士用 Not necessarily 先否定男士\"学得快\"的说\n法，紧接着抛出 Tablets have too many distractions——否定/转折之后亮出的才是她真\n正要说的弊端，distractions 这个词直接对应\"分心\"。\n【替换】Tablets have too many distractions ↔ get distracted more easily\n\"有太多让人分心的东西\"=\"更容易分心\"\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音说 people who read printed text comprehend more（读纸质的人理解得更多），A\n借 comprehend 这个词编成 They may have trouble comprehending texts.（理解文字\n有困难）。原文是\"读纸质理解更多\"的对比，不是说学生用平板\"看不懂\"，听到\ncomprehend 就选即落陷阱。\n借用原文：people who read printed text comprehend more\nB) 未提及\nThey may encounter technological problems. 说学生会遇到技术问题，但女士谈的是分\n心和纸质阅读理解更好，全程没说\"学生用平板会碰到技术故障\"，是凭\"用电子设备会出故\n障\"的常识补出来的。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音说学生可能 pay attention to apps, games and websites instead of their teachers\n（去看应用游戏而非听老师），C 借 teachers 偷换成 They may pay less respect to\nteachers.（不那么尊重老师）。原文说的是\"注意力不在老师身上\"，不是\"不尊重老师\"，听\n到 teachers 就选即中招。\n借用原文：pay attention to apps, games and websites instead of their teachers", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-15", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 15, "stem": "What does the woman say about making electronic readers?", "options": ["A) It generates a great deal of electronic garbage.", "B) It does a lot of damage to the environment.", "C) It emits huge amounts of harmful radiation.", "D) It accelerates the exhaustion of rare minerals."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It does a lot of damage to the environment.\n【定位】Actually, manufacturing tablets is environmentally destructive and dangerous to human\nhealth. A lot of minerals are extracted from the earth to make electronic readers. It does far\nmore damage to the environment.\n实际上，制造平板对环境有破坏性，也危害人体健康。要从地里开采大量矿物来制造电子阅\n读器。它对环境造成的破坏要大得多。\n【信号】末句 It does far more damage 是收束结论。女士这段先说制造平板 environmentally\ndestructive，列举健康危害和开采矿物，最后用 It does far more damage to the\nenvironment 一句收束——段末的总括性结论句正是她对\"制造电子阅读器\"的核心表态，\n是答案句。\n【替换】It does far more damage to the environment ↔ does a lot of damage to the environment\n\"破坏环境大得多\"=\"对环境造成很大破坏\"\n【排除】A) 未提及\nIt generates a great deal of electronic garbage. 说产生大量电子垃圾，但女士谈的是健\n康危害、开采矿物、破坏环境，没有一句提到\"电子垃圾/garbage\"，是把环保话题往\"电子\n垃圾\"这个常见印象上靠的未提及项。\nC) 未提及\nIt emits huge amounts of harmful radiation. 说释放大量有害辐射，听上去很吓人，但女\n士只说 destructive、dangerous to health、extract minerals，从没说\"辐\n射/radiation\"，是凭\"电子产品有辐射\"的印象编的未提及项。\nD) 原词陷阱\n录音说 A lot of minerals are extracted from the earth to make electronic readers（开\n采大量矿物来造电子阅读器），D 借 minerals 编成 It accelerates the exhaustion of rare\nminerals.（加速稀有矿物枯竭）。原文说的是\"开采矿物\"这一事实，没说\"加速枯竭\"这个后\n果，且本题要的是女士的总括结论（破坏环境），D 是中途细节的过度引申。\n借用原文：A lot of minerals are extracted from the earth to make electronic readers", "points": ["听力·篇章", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-16", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 16, "stem": "What does the passage say we tend to do every day?", "options": ["A) Communicate with our coworkers.", "B) Encounter people in different places.", "C) Judge people based on our first impressions.", "D) Engage in a variety of psychological activities."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） Judge people based on our first impressions.\n【定位】Consider how often you make this kind of judgment every day. When you meet with a new\ncoworker, you immediately begin to develop an initial impression of this person.\n想想你每天多频繁地做出这种评判。当你见到一位新同事，你立刻就开始形成对这个人的最\n初印象。\n【信号】every day 原词复现+举例佐证。录音用 Consider how often you make this kind of\njudgment every day 直接点出我们每天都在做的这件事就是 judgment（评判），随后用\n见新同事就 develop an initial impression 的例子佐证，every day 与题干同词，是答案句\n的定位信号。\n【替换】this kind of judgment ↔ Judge people based on our first impressions\njudgment 名词→Judge 动词同根\ndevelop an initial impression ↔ based on our first impressions\ninitial impression=first impression 同义\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音确实出现 When you meet with a new coworker，A 项 Communicate with our\ncoworkers. 借了 coworker 这个原词，但录音说的是见到新同事时会形成印象，不是与同\n事沟通，盗词却答非所问。\n借用原文：When you meet with a new coworker\nB) 偷换概念\n录音有 When you visit the grocery store 这个去不同地方的例子，B 项 Encounter\npeople in different places. 借此包装成在不同地方遇见人，但录音此处讲的仍是对收银员\n形成评判，不是强调遇见人这件事本身，属把例子细节偷换成选项主语。\n借用原文：When you visit the grocery store\nD) 过度推断\n录音讲的是一种具体行为——做评判、形成印象，D 项 Engage in a variety of\npsychological activities. 用 a variety of psychological activities 把它说得又大又泛，范\n围被夸大，不是录音点名的那一件每天做的事。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-17", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 17, "stem": "What do we learn about person perception from this passage?", "options": ["A) It is an objective evaluation of a person's character.", "B) It is a mental process influenced by many factors.", "C) It contributes to the formation of personal traits.", "D) It varies greatly among different social groups."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is a mental process influenced by many factors.\n【定位】Obviously, person perception is a very subjective process that can be affected by a number of\nvariables, including the characteristics of the person you are observing, the context of the\nsituation, your own personal traits, and your past experiences.\n显然，识人知觉是一个非常主观的过程，会受到许多变量的影响，包括你所观察对象的特\n征、情境的背景、你自身的性格特质，以及你过往的经历。\n【信号】Obviously 强调+定性句。录音用 Obviously 加重语气引出对该概念的定性，紧接着 a\nvery subjective process that can be affected by a number of variables 直接说它是主观\n的、受许多变量影响，这是专门给 person perception 定性的答案句。\n【替换】a very subjective process ↔ It is a mental process influenced by many factors\nsubjective process=mental process\naffected by a number of variables ↔ influenced by many factors\naffected by variables=influenced by factors\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音明说它是 a very subjective process（主观过程），A 项 It is an objective\nevaluation of a person's character. 却说成 objective（客观），把性质说反了，且\nevaluation、character 也只是蹭话题词。\n借用原文：a very subjective process\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音里 your own personal traits 是影响 person perception 的因素之一，C 项 It\ncontributes to the formation of personal traits. 却反过来说它造就了性格特质，借了\npersonal traits 这个原词，但把因果方向整个调反了。\n借用原文：your own personal traits\nD) 未提及\nD 项 It varies greatly among different social groups. 说它在不同社会群体间差异巨大，\n但录音讲的是它受个人层面的多种变量影响，从未提到社会群体之间的差异，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-18", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 18, "stem": "What is the problem with using social categorization in person perception?", "options": ["A) It can lead to incorrect judgments.", "B) It can cause mistrust among people.", "C) It can result in instant losses.", "D) It can give rise to gender bias."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） It can lead to incorrect judgments.\n【定位】Problems with this technique include the fact that it can lead to errors and prejudice.\n这种方法的问题包括：它会导致错误和偏见。\n【信号】Problems 原词+lead to errors。录音用 Problems with this technique 直接对应题干的\nproblem，紧跟 it can lead to errors and prejudice 点出弊端就是导致错误和偏见，\nProblems 一词与题干同义，是定位答案句的强信号。\n【替换】it can lead to errors ↔ It can lead to incorrect judgments\nerrors=incorrect judgments\n【排除】B) 未提及\nB 项 It can cause mistrust among people. 说会造成人际不信任，但录音讲的弊端是\nerrors and prejudice（错误与偏见），从没说到不信任，属凭空添加的后果。\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音例子结尾说被偷了钱包 leading to the loss of your wallet，C 项 It can result in\ninstant losses. 借 loss 这个原词包装成即时损失，但那只是一个具体例子的细节，本题问\n的是 social categorization 的整体弊端（错误与偏见），盗词答非所问，且 instant 也是录\n音没有的。\n借用原文：leading to the loss of your wallet\nD) 偷换概念\n录音说弊端含 prejudice（偏见），D 项 It can give rise to gender bias. 借偏见这层意思\n却收窄成性别偏见，录音例子讲的是凭老妇与壮汉的外貌分类，从没限定为性别偏见，属以\n偏概全。\n借用原文：prejudice", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-19", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 19, "stem": "What does the new study suggest about young people today and those from the previous generation?", "options": ["A) Both groups spend a lot of time on mobile devices.", "B) Both groups attach importance to social connections.", "C) They are equally competent in using new technology.", "D) They are similar in terms of social skills."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） They are similar in terms of social skills.\n【定位】Despite smartphones and social media, young people today are as socially competent as those\nfrom the previous generation.\n尽管有智能手机和社交媒体，今天的年轻人在社交能力上与上一代人不相上下。\n【信号】让步 Despite 后才是真意。答案信号是让步词 Despite smartphones and social media\n——让步状语先抛出一个看似负面的前提，真正的研究结论在它之后的主句 young people\ntoday are as socially competent as those from the previous generation，这种\nDespite／Although 后的主句正是篇章听力的高频落点。\n【替换】as socially competent as those from the previous generation ↔\nThey are similar in terms of social skills.\nas…as 等同改写成 similar\n【排除】A) 原词陷阱\n录音开头确实念到 smartphones and social media，A 项 Both groups spend a lot of\ntime on mobile devices. 借这个原词诱你选，但原文只说尽管有手机、社交能力照样相\n当，从没说两代人都花大量时间在设备上，属听到原词就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：smartphones and social media\nB) 未提及\nB 项 Both groups attach importance to social connections. 说两代都重视社交联系，但\n原文只比较社交能力强弱（competent），从未说他们重视或看重社交，研究里没有这层意\n思。\nC) 偷换概念\n录音提到 mobile devices were widespread，C 项 They are equally competent in\nusing new technology. 借 technology 与 competent 两个耳熟词诱选，但原文说的是社\n交能力（socially competent）相当，不是使用技术的能力相当，把社交能力偷换成了用技\n术的能力。\n借用原文：mobile devices were widespread", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-20", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 20, "stem": "What did the study find about children who accessed social networking sites many times a day?", "options": ["A) Their social skills were negatively affected.", "B) Their school performance was slightly lower.", "C) Their emotions were much harder to regulate.", "D) Their relations with peers were badly strained."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题） Their social skills were negatively affected.\n【定位】There was one exception: Social skills were slightly lower for children who accessed online\ngames and social networking sites many times a day.\n有一个例外：对于每天多次玩网络游戏和上社交网站的孩子，他们的社交能力略低一些。\n【信号】例外 one exception 即落点。答案信号是转折式提示词 There was one exception——前\n文一直说社交能力保持不变或提升，唯独这个 exception 后面交代了负面发现 Social skills\nwere slightly lower，例外句是典型的答案落点。\n【替换】Social skills were slightly lower ↔ Their social skills were negatively affected.\n变低概括为受负面影响\n【排除】B) 原词陷阱\n录音里 slightly lower 修饰的是 social skills（社交能力），B 项 Their school\nperformance was slightly lower. 原封不动搬来 slightly lower，却把对象换成学业表现，\n原文从没说成绩略低，属听到 slightly lower 就想选的陷阱。\n借用原文：Social skills were slightly lower\nC) 张冠李戴\n录音在第 3 段提过 regulate their temper，C 项 Their emotions were much harder to\nregulate. 借 regulate 诱选，但那是第 3 段讲两组孩子在自控上相似时的话，跟第 4 段每\n天多次上网那群孩子无关，且程度被夸成 much harder，与原文 slightly 相反。\n借用原文：regulate their temper\nD) 过度推断\nD 项 Their relations with peers were badly strained. 说同伴关系严重紧张，原文既没说\n这群孩子的同伴关系受损，也没用 badly 这种重程度词（原文是 slightly），属夸大加无中\n生有。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-21", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 21, "stem": "What is adults' worry about technological change?", "options": ["A) It may pose a threat to their children's safety.", "B) It may affect society's traditional values.", "C) It may hurt their relations with children.", "D) It may change their children's ethical values."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It may hurt their relations with children.\n【定位】Adults are worried when technological change starts to undermine traditional relationships,\nparticularly the parent-child relationship.\n当科技变化开始侵蚀传统关系、尤其是亲子关系时，成年人会感到担忧。\n【信号】worried 直陈担忧 + particularly 点重点。答案信号是表担忧的谓语 Adults are\nworried，其担忧对象由 undermine traditional relationships 给出，再由强调词\nparticularly the parent-child relationship 把重点收窄到亲子关系——particularly 后面的\n限定正是答案要的具体落点。\n【替换】undermine traditional relationships, particularly the parent-child relationship ↔\nIt may hurt their relations with children.\nundermine=hurt，parent-child=with children\n【排除】A) 未提及\nA 项 It may pose a threat to their children's safety. 借耳熟的 children 诱选，但原文成\n年人担忧的是亲子关系被侵蚀，从没提孩子的人身安全（safety），属新增内容。\nB) 偷换概念\n录音念过 traditional relationships 与第 6 段的 moral panic，B 项 It may affect\nsociety's traditional values. 借 traditional 诱选，但原文担忧的是传统关系\n（relationships），不是社会传统价值（values），把关系偷换成了价值观。\n借用原文：traditional relationships\nD) 过度推断\n第 6 段提到 moral panic，D 项 It may change their children's ethical values. 借这条道\n德暗线诱选，但 moral panic 指成年人面对新技术的恐慌情绪，原文从未说科技会改变孩子\n的道德／伦理价值观，属由 moral 一词过度推断。\n借用原文：moral panic", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-22", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 22, "stem": "What does the speaker say about inspiration from consuming others' ideas and success stories?", "options": ["A) It is motivating.", "B) It is passive.", "C) It is incredible.", "D) It is impracticable."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） It is passive.\n【定位】The problem is that consuming the success and ideas of others is passive inspiration.\n问题在于，消费他人的成功与想法，是一种被动的灵感。\n【信号】下定义句 is + 定性词。这句用 consuming the success and ideas of others is passive\ninspiration 的 \"A is B\" 结构，直接给 \"消费他人成功\" 贴上 passive inspiration 的标签，\n是典型的下定义信号，定性题答案就藏在这种 \"...is...\" 的判断里。\n【替换】passive inspiration ↔ It is passive.\n原词原义直接对应\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音把 consuming 来的灵感判为 passive（被动），而 It is motivating. 说它有激励性，\n恰与原文相反，是把被动说成主动激励。\n借用原文：passive inspiration\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 P1 确实出现过 incredible videos, articles and news stories，incredible 是用来形\n容那些视频文章 \"很精彩\"，并不是给 passive inspiration 的定性；选项 It is incredible. 盗\n这个原词来骗 \"听到就选\"。\n借用原文：incredible videos, articles and news stories\nD) 未提及\n录音从未说 consuming 来的 inspiration 是 impracticable（不切实际的），原文只说它\npassive、不能代替自己创造，并没有 \"不可行\" 这层意思，属凭空添加。", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-23", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 23, "stem": "What do we learn from the passage about active inspiration?", "options": ["A) It results in short-term excitement.", "B) It helps us avoid making mistakes.", "C) It breeds long-term passion and enthusiasm.", "D) It is bound to help us achieve greater success."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题） It breeds long-term passion and enthusiasm.\n【定位】Furthermore, active inspiration is what results in long-term passion and enthusiasm.\n此外，主动的灵感正是带来长久热情与激情的东西。\n【信号】递进 Furthermore 后给定论。Furthermore, active inspiration is what results in long-\nterm passion and enthusiasm 由 Furthermore 引出对 active inspiration 的进一步定\n论，递进词后常接观点落点；同时 results in 是因果信号，直接给出它的结果。\n【替换】results in long-term passion and enthusiasm ↔ It breeds long-term passion and enthusiasm.\nresults in 同义替换为 breeds\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音说 short-term 的是 watching 别人成功带来的兴奋——Watching someone else's\nsuccess might leave you feeling excited for a few minutes，那是 passive 一侧的效\n果；It results in short-term excitement. 把这份 \"短暂兴奋\" 安到 active inspiration 头\n上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：Watching someone else's success might leave you feeling excited for a few minutes\nB) 与原文相反\n录音讲 active inspiration 包含 making mistakes（犯错）才认识自我，从没说它能 avoid\nmaking mistakes（避免犯错），It helps us avoid making mistakes. 把原文里的 \"犯错\"\n反着安成 \"避免犯错\"，原文无此说法。\n借用原文：making mistakes that we discover who we are\nD) 过度推断\n录音只说 active inspiration 带来长期热情、给你 power，并没有保证它 \"必然带来更大成\n功\"；It is bound to help us achieve greater success. 中 bound to（必定）把效果夸成\n必然成功，推过了头。\n借用原文：active inspiration is what results in long-term passion and enthusiasm", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-24", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 24, "stem": "What does the passage say passive inspiration can do?", "options": ["A) Drive us forward.", "B) Bring us power.", "C) Spur us to action.", "D) Give us ideas."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题） Give us ideas.\n【定位】Passive inspiration can give you ideas, but active inspiration will give you power.\n被动的灵感能给你想法，但主动的灵感会给你力量。\n【信号】对比句 but 两侧分派功能。Passive inspiration can give you ideas, but active\ninspiration will give you power 用 but 把两种 inspiration 的功能并排对照，passive 给\nideas、active 给 power；对比句两侧的分工正是答案点，须按主语取属于 passive 的那\n半句。\n【替换】Passive inspiration can give you ideas ↔ Give us ideas.\ngive you ideas 改 give us ideas\n【排除】A) 张冠李戴\n录音 P4 说 creating, producing, and experimenting is what drives you forward，drive\nforward 是 active 一侧（创造、试验）的功能；Drive us forward. 把它安到 passive 头\n上，张冠李戴。\n借用原文：creating, producing, and experimenting is what drives you forward\nB) 原词陷阱\n对比句明说 active inspiration will give you power，给 power 的是 active；Bring us\npower. 盗用原词 power 却把它派给 passive，正中 but 后半句被对调的陷阱。\n借用原文：active inspiration will give you power\nC) 原词陷阱\n录音 P3 出现过 taking action and applying a new idea to your life，action 是讲 active\ninspiration 的语境，且原文并无 \"passive 能 spur us to action\" 的说法；Spur us to\naction. 借 action 这个原词制造熟悉感，却答非所问。\n借用原文：taking action and applying a new idea to your life", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-l-25", "type": "listening", "typeZh": "听力", "qno": 25, "stem": "Where does the best inspiration come from according to the passage?", "options": ["A) Listening to success stories.", "B) Applying ideas to one's life.", "C) Following the advice of experts.", "D) Consuming the world around us."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题） Applying ideas to one's life.\n【定位】The best inspiration comes from the application of ideas, not the consumption of them.\n最好的灵感来自对想法的应用，而非对它们的消费。\n【信号】结尾总括句 the best...comes from。The best inspiration comes from the application\nof ideas, not the consumption of them 是末段收束的总括定调句，the best...comes\nfrom 直接给出结论，且用 not 把对立的 consumption 排掉，是篇章结尾的答案落点\n信号。\n【替换】the application of ideas ↔ Applying ideas to one's life.\n名词 application 还原为动词 applying\n【排除】A) 与原文相反\n录音讲的是 application 对 consumption，并没说最佳灵感来自 \"listening to success\nstories（听成功故事）\"，反倒把 \"听别人成功\" 归为 passive；Listening to success\nstories. 与结论相悖。\n借用原文：Hearing about other people's success isn't the same as creating your own\nC) 未提及\n全文从未提到 \"following the advice of experts（听从专家建议）\"，没有任何对应句，属\n凭空添加的来源。\nD) 与原文相反\n结尾 not the consumption of them 明确用 not 把 consumption 排在最佳来源之外，\nConsuming the world around us. 盗用录音确实念过的 consuming/consumption，却恰\n恰选了被否定的那一极。\n借用原文：not the consumption of them", "points": ["听力·讲座", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-26", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 26, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E) fortune\n名词\n【词性槽】fame and ＿＿：and 并列一个名词\n【依据】fame and fortune＝名利，是固定搭配\nfame and fortune（名与利）是英语里押头韵的经典固定搭配。句意是“92% 的人认为别人\n最看重名和利”，fortune＝财富，与 fame 并列恰成“名利”，且呼应全文“公众以为成功＝名\n利、实则不然”的对比主旨。\n【竞争词】J) profession “职业”与 fame 不构成习惯并列\nprofession 是“职业”，fame and profession 不是惯用搭配，也偏离“别人只在乎名利”这层\n意思。\nK) purpose 正话反说，purpose 属“真实成功观”，留给 31\npurpose（人生意义）恰恰是文中人们“自己”真正看重的东西，与“别人只看重 fame and …”的语\n境唱反调；它是 31 空 having a __(31)__ in life 的答案，留着它。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-27", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 27, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "M", "analysis": "【答案】M) released\n动词\n【词性槽】newly 与 study 之间缺过去分词作定语\n【依据】“新近发布的研究”是固定说法\n研究/报告与“发布”搭配：a newly released study＝一项新近发布的研究，正好接上一句结\n论的来源 This is according to the newly __(27)__ study。release a study 是学术语境里\n的常见表达。\n【竞争词】C) acquiring -ing 主动义，与 study 不搭\nacquiring 是现在分词，表“正在获得……的”主动义，study 不会主动去“获得”什么，a newly\nacquiring study 讲不通。\nD) bothered 词义不对，研究不会“被困扰”\nbothered 意为“被困扰的”，a newly bothered study（新近被困扰的研究）语义荒谬，与“发布\n研究”的语境完全无关。\nF) fulfilling 义为“充实的”，且后面 28 题更需要它\nfulfilling 表“令人有成就感的”，修饰 study 不通；且它在下文 32 空有更贴的去处，留着它。\nL) ranked 搭配不成立，留给 33 题\na newly ranked study（新近被排名的研究）不通，ranked 表“排名、位列”，是 33 空 Being a\nparent __(33)__ very high 的固定用法，留着它。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-28", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 28, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D) bothered\n动词\n【词性槽】was ＿＿ by：被动结构里的过去分词\n【依据】was bothered by＝被……所困扰/感到不安\nbe bothered by 是固定搭配，意为“被……困扰、对……感到不安”。Smith 对过去研\n究“assumed what people will care about”（想当然地假设人们在乎什么）这一做法感到\n不满/困扰，正是他另起炉灶做新研究的动机，与下句 his team \"went the __(29)__\ndirection\"（走相反方向）顺理成章地衔接。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-29", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 29, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I) opposite\n名词\n【词性槽】the ＿＿ direction：the 与 direction 间作定语的名词\n【依据】the opposite direction＝相反的方向\ngo the opposite direction＝“走相反的方向”，正好承接上句 Smith 对旧研究“想当然假设”\n的不满 he was __(28)__ by how past studies ...——所以他的团队反其道而行，by\nspending years carrying out individual interviews and group surveys to see what\npeople really talk about，用实地访谈去问人们真正在乎什么。opposite 在此作定语修\n饰 direction。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-30", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 30, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H) literally\n副词\n【词性槽】主语与动词 studied 之间插一个状语\n【依据】literally 强调“确确实实、名副其实地”\nSmith 是研究个体差异的科学家，literally studied individuality for a living＝“确实是以研\n究个体差异为生”，literally 在这里起强调作用，凸显“连研究这行的他都没料到”的转折，与\n后半句 and even he was surprised 呼应。\n【竞争词】B) accordingly 表“相应地”，无前因可承接\naccordingly 意为“因此、据此”，需要前文有可据以行动的原因，这里只是陈述他的职业，用它\n逻辑上接不上；它是 34 空的答案。\nN) similarly 表“同样地”，需有可类比对象\nsimilarly 表“与前述类似地”，需要前文有一个可对照的相似情形，此处没有，用它前后无可比\n之处。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-31", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 31, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K) purpose\n名词\n【词性槽】a ＿＿ in life：不定冠词 a 后接名词\n【依据】have a purpose in life＝人生有目标/意义\nhave a purpose in life 是地道搭配，意为“人生有意义、有使命感”。下一句把它换说成\nthat prioritization 并与 stable incomes than __(32)__ personal missions（个人使命）呼\n应——purpose 正是“personal mission”这层“人生意义”的概念，与全文“年轻人更看重人生\n意义而非名利”的发现一致。\n【竞争词】J) profession “职业”不与 in life 搭、也偏离主旨\na profession in life 不是惯用表达，且“有一份职业”讲的是谋生，与本段强调年轻人看重“人生意\n义”而非稳定收入的对比相悖。", "points": ["选词填空", "名词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-32", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 32, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F) fulfilling\n形容词\n【词性槽】＿＿ personal missions：名词短语前作定语\n【依据】fulfilling＝令人有成就感的，与 missions 同向\nfulfilling personal missions＝“令人充实／有成就感的个人使命”。本段对比的是“老一辈更\n看重稳定收入 (stable incomes) vs 年轻人更看重有意义的个人使命”，fulfilling 这一正向、\n表“成就感”的形容词，恰好刻画年轻人追求的那种“有意义的”使命，与 31 空 purpose 一脉\n相承。\n【竞争词】I) opposite “相反的”修饰 missions 语义不成立\nopposite 表“相反的、对立的”，opposite personal missions（相反的个人使命）讲不通，且与\n对比逻辑无关。\nO) wrong 感情色彩为贬义，与正向追求冲突\nwrong 是“错误的”，带贬义；这里要正面描述年轻人向往的使命，用 wrong personal missions\n（错误的个人使命）感情色彩相反、逻辑矛盾。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 O) —— wrong 词性对、又被开头“误解”一词带偏。32 题 than\n__(32)__ personal missions 处，wrong 是形容词、能修饰 missions，加上首句 might be\ntotally misguided 谈“错误认知”，容易让人顺手填“wrong”。防坑：本空要正面描述年轻人\n追求的“有意义的”使命，需褒义词 fulfilling；wrong 是贬义，感情色彩一冲突就该排除。", "points": ["选词填空", "形容词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-33", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 33, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L) ranked\n动词\n【词性槽】主语 Being a parent 之后缺谓语动词\n【依据】rank high＝排名靠前、位列很高\nrank high 是固定搭配，意为“排名很高、位居前列”，正好接 very high：Being a parent\n__(33)__ very high＝“为人父母在所有受访者的优先项里排得很高”，与上一句 Other\ntrends included an emphasis on the importance of parenting.（强调为人父母之重要）\n一脉相承。\n【竞争词】C) acquiring -ing 形式不能单独作谓语\nacquiring 是现在分词，单独不能充当谓语动词；且“获得很高”与 very high 也搭不上。\nD) bothered 词义与“排名很高”无关\nbothered 意为“困扰”，Being a parent bothered very high 既不通顺也无意义。\nF) fulfilling 义为“充实的”，与 high 不搭，留给 32\nfulfilling 是形容词“令人有成就感的”，不能跟 very high 构成“排名很高”，且它在 32 空有更贴\n的位置。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-34", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 34, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B) accordingly\n副词\n【词性槽】句末修饰动词短语 take note 的状语\n【依据】据研究结论“相应地”采取行动\n前文摆出研究发现（人们真正看重的是人生意义、为人父母等），Smith 希望机构 take\nnote of these insights accordingly＝“相应地、据此去关注这些洞见”，accordingly 承接\n“根据上述发现 → 据此行动”的因果逻辑，与末段 they'll need to understand \"what the\nAmerican public highly prioritizes\" 的导向一致。\n【竞争词】N) similarly 表“同样地”，缺可类比对象\nsimilarly 需要前文给出一个相似情形来类比，这里是“据研究结论行动”的因果关系而非类比，用\n它逻辑断裂。", "points": ["选词填空", "副词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-c-35", "type": "cloze", "typeZh": "选词填空", "qno": 35, "stem": "", "options": ["A) accommodate", "B) accordingly", "C) acquiring", "D) bothered", "E) fortune", "F) fulfilling", "G) identify", "H) literally", "I) opposite", "J) profession", "K) purpose", "L) ranked", "M) released", "N) similarly", "O) wrong"], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A) accommodate\n动词\n【词性槽】to better ＿＿ people：to 后跟动词原形\n【依据】accommodate people＝顾及、服务好人群的需求\n末段说机构要了解 \"what the American public highly prioritizes\"（美国公众最看重什\n么），目的是 to better accommodate people＝“更好地顾及/迁就人们（的真实需\n求）”，accommodate 在此为“适应、满足某群体需求”，与“先懂公众所重、再据此服务好\n他们”的逻辑严丝合缝。\n【竞争词】G) identify 义为“识别”，与“服务好人们”指向不符\nidentify people 是“辨认出人/确认身份”，而本句要表达的是机构去“迎合、服务好”公众的需\n求，不是去“识别”他们，词义指向不对。\n【易错】最具迷惑性的错误词是 G) —— identify 词性全对、也能接 people，最易误选。35 题 to\nbetter __(35)__ people in the U.S. 处，identify 与正确答案 accommodate 同为及物动\n词、宾语都能是 people，光看语法分不开。防坑：回到句子目的——机构是“懂了公众所重\n之后更好地服务/顾及他们（accommodate）”，不是“识别（identify）”他们，靠语义指向\n切开两词。", "points": ["选词填空", "动词"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-36", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 36, "stem": "Kelli Harding also distinguishes herself by her literary talent.", "options": null, "answer": "E", "analysis": "【答案】E 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】literary talent 文学才能\n【定位】If Kelli stands out, it's because she is also a gifted writer.\n如果说 Kelli 出类拔萃，那是因为她还是一位有天赋的作家。\n【改写】distinguishes herself ↔ Kelli stands out\n出类拔萃的同义替换\nher literary talent ↔ she is also a gifted writer\n身份化为才能的名词化", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-37", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 37, "stem": "Kelli Harding doesn't think America's medical model is sufficient for patients who need help most.", "options": null, "answer": "K", "analysis": "【答案】K 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】medical model is sufficient 医学模式是否够用\n【定位】In my practice, the sickest people I see often share similar backgrounds: loneliness, abuse,\npoverty, or discrimination. For them, the medical model isn't enough.\n在我的临床实践中，我见到的病情最重的病人往往有相似的背景：孤独、受虐、贫困或被歧\n视。对他们而言，单靠医学模式是不够的。\n【改写】doesn't think America's medical model is sufficient ↔ the medical model isn't enough\n含蓄否定 = 直接否定 + 反义\npatients who need help most ↔\nthe sickest people I see often share similar backgrounds: loneliness, abuse, poverty, or\ndiscrimination\n最需要帮助的人 ↔ 病情最重的弱势人群\n【辨邻】 I I 段只描述她\"不肯随大流\"，并未出现 medical model is sufficient 等价表述，差在没\n有这条判断句。\nKelli Harding stands apart from the crowd calling for quick fixes, the simpler the better\nJ J 段缺少 patients who need help most 这一对应群体的明确指代，主张层级与陈述\n句不在同一颗粒度上。\nThere's a social dimension to health that we've completely overlooked in our efforts to find the best\nand most cutting-edge medical care", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-38", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 38, "stem": "Kelli Harding differs from those seeking quick and simple solutions to America's medical problems.", "options": null, "answer": "I", "analysis": "【答案】I 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】quick and simple solutions 快速简单方案\n【定位】Amid the political noise about Obamacare, Medicare, Medicaid, health insurance and\nthieving pharmaceutical companies, Kelli Harding stands apart from the crowd calling for\nquick fixes, the simpler the better.\n在围绕奥巴马医改、医保、医补、医疗保险以及黑心药企的政治喧嚣里，Kelli Harding 与\n那些呼吁\"越简单越好\"的速效方案者拉开了距离。\n【改写】differs from those seeking quick and simple solutions ↔\nstands apart from the crowd calling for quick fixes, the simpler the better\n立场拉开距离的同义替换\nAmerica's medical problems ↔\nObamacare, Medicare, Medicaid, health insurance and thieving pharmaceutical companies\n上位概括\n【辨邻】 K K 批的是 assembly-line medicine，I 批的是\"求快求简者\"。陈述句 those seeking\nquick and simple solutions 对位的是后者。\nassembly-line medicine where patients were treated mostly as pieces of flesh\nL L 强调的是不去攻击医学体制，Q38 强调的是与速效派拉开距离，方向相反。\nKelli wastes no time taking potshots at the medical establishment", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-39", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 39, "stem": "Kelli Harding was a participant in a summer course the author taught.", "options": null, "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】summer course the author taught 作者教的暑期课\n【定位】So it was when a recent letter came from Kelli Harding, a student 21 years ago in my Peace\nStudies summer course in Washington.\n前段时间收到 Kelli Harding 来信时也是如此——她是我 21 年前在华盛顿开设的 Peace\nStudies 暑期课的学生。\n【改写】Kelli Harding was a participant ↔ Kelli Harding, a student 21 years ago\n参与者 ↔ 学生\na summer course the author taught ↔ my Peace Studies summer course in Washington\n所有格暗含\"我教\"\n【辨邻】 C C 段没有 a summer course the author taught 的等价表述，缺关键指纹。\nHer year-long service included comforting AIDS patients at a free health clinic and delivering\nmeals to the homebound", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-40", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 40, "stem": "According to Kelli Harding, scientific advances have not made Americans healthier, nor prolonged their life.", "options": null, "answer": "F", "analysis": "【答案】F 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】scientific advances 与 Americans healthier 的对比\n【定位】\"Despite our scientific progress,\" she writes, \"Americans are remarkably unhealthy. In 2016,\nthe United States ranked forty-third in the world for life expectancy.\"\n\"尽管我们的科学不断进步\"，她写道，\"美国人却惊人地不健康。2016 年，美国的人均预期\n寿命在世界排名第 43。\"\n【改写】scientific advances have not made Americans healthier ↔\nDespite our scientific progress,\" she writes, \"Americans are remarkably unhealthy\n尽管 A 仍 B = A 没让 B 反过来\nnor prolonged their life ↔ the United States ranked forty-third in the world for life expectancy\n排名 43 = 没延长寿命\n【辨邻】 G G 段的对象是实验中的兔子，缺 Americans 与 scientific advances 的对位。\ntwo month-long medical experiments to test the effects of eating a high-fat diet and the connections\nbetween cholesterol and heart disease", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-41", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 41, "stem": "The author was deeply moved by what Kelli Harding wrote about her current life.", "options": null, "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】deeply moved 被深深打动\n【定位】Kelli's letter, a literate update on both her personal and professional life, touched my heart,\nand especially so when saying that two decades later she still has the course text, \"Solutions\nto Violence,\" and that \"it remains one of my favorite possessions.\"\nKelli 的来信，一份既谈个人生活也谈职业生涯的文采斐然的近况报告，触动了我；尤其当她\n写到 20 年过去她仍然保留那本课程教材《Solutions to Violence》、并称\"它仍是我最珍爱\n的物件之一\"时。\n【改写】deeply moved ↔ touched my heart\n心动的主被动替换\nwhat Kelli Harding wrote about her current life ↔\na literate update on both her personal and professional life\n更新 ↔ 现在的生活\n【辨邻】 A A 段没有 what Kelli Harding wrote 这一具体对象，只是泛说老师们的情感时刻。\nfew moments stir the emotions as deeply as when former students reappear years and often\ndecades later", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-42", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 42, "stem": "George Engel, in treating his patients, not only looks into their symptoms but also into things like the emotional support they receive.", "options": null, "answer": "L", "analysis": "【答案】L 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】George Engel 专名\n【定位】Instead, she remains positive, holding up for praise one of her medical school professors,\nGeorge Engel, \"who always noticed not just a patient's physical condition but little\ndetails about her life, such as if she had family pictures up in her hospital room or\nflowers delivered.\"\n相反，她保持正向视角，把她医学院的一位老师 George Engel 当作正面榜样：他不仅关\n注病人的身体状况，还留意她生活里的小细节，比如病房里是否摆着家人的合影、是否有人\n送花。\n【改写】not only looks into their symptoms but also into things like the emotional support they receive ↔\nalways noticed not just a patient's physical condition but little details about her life, such as if\nshe had family pictures up in her hospital room or flowers delivered\n症状 ↔ 身体状况；情感支持 ↔ 家人合影/送花\nin treating his patients ↔ He'd sit down to talk with the patient\n看病 ↔ 与病人坐下交谈\n【辨邻】 K K 段不含 George Engel 的等价指代，主语错位。\nTo properly care for patients, we also need to care about the lives of the people getting the care", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-43", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 43, "stem": "According to Kelli Harding, rabbits' health had more to do with humans' kindness to them than their diet or genetics.", "options": null, "answer": "H", "analysis": "【答案】H 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】rabbits 与 kindness 的因果\n【定位】It wasn't diet or genetics that made a difference in which rabbits got sick and which stayed\nhealthy. It was kindness.\n决定哪些兔子生病、哪些保持健康的，并不是饮食或基因，而是善意。\n【改写】rabbits' health had more to do with humans' kindness to them ↔\nIt wasn't diet or genetics that made a difference in which rabbits got sick and which stayed\nhealthy. It was kindness.\n强调句 = 比较句的等价\nthan their diet or genetics ↔ It wasn't diet or genetics\n否定项 = 比较中的次要项\nhumans' kindness ↔ she gave them love\n善意 ↔ 给予爱\n【辨邻】 G G 段只交代现象，没出现 kindness 等价表述，差在没结论。\nYet one group had 60% fewer of them", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-44", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 44, "stem": "What Kelli Harding went through in Washington changed her life.", "options": null, "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】Washington 经历改变人生\n【定位】The Washington experience, which Kelli would later call \"transformative,\" was the fuel that\ncarried her into medicine to earn a master's degree in public health from Columbia University\nand a medical degree from the University of Rochester, and almost two decades of practice as\nan emergency-room psychiatrist at New York-Presbyterian Hospital and a clinical professor\nof psychiatry at Columbia University Irving Medical Center.\nKelli 后来称之为\"改变人生\"（transformative）的华盛顿经历，正是把她推进医学界的燃料\n——让她在哥伦比亚大学拿下公共卫生硕士、在罗彻斯特大学拿到医学学位，并在纽约长老\n会医院做了近二十年的急诊精神科医生，又在哥大欧文医学中心担任临床精神病学教授。\n【改写】What Kelli Harding went through in Washington ↔\nThe Washington experience, which Kelli would later call \"transformative\"\n经历的同义替换\nchanged her life ↔ transformative\n形容词 ↔ 动词短语\n【辨邻】 B B 段没有 transformative 等价表述，只是介绍课程与志愿者身份。\na student 21 years ago in my Peace Studies summer course in Washington", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-m-45", "type": "match", "typeZh": "信息匹配", "qno": 45, "stem": "A social aspect to health has not been taken into account in trying to provide the best medical care.", "options": null, "answer": "J", "analysis": "【答案】J 段（强锚点）\n【锚点】social aspect to health 健康的社会维度\n【定位】There's a social dimension to health that we've completely overlooked in our efforts to find\nthe best and most cutting-edge medical care …\n在我们追求最尖端医疗的过程中，完全忽视了健康的一个社会维度……\n【改写】A social aspect to health ↔ There's a social dimension to health\naspect = dimension\nhas not been taken into account ↔ we've completely overlooked\n忽视的同义被动化\nin trying to provide the best medical care ↔\nin our efforts to find the best and most cutting-edge medical care\n介词短语原样替换\n【辨邻】 K K 段没有 social dimension 的等价术语，差在术语缺失。\nseemingly irrelevant factors, such as their family relationships or their educational levels", "points": ["信息匹配·同义替换"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-46", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 46, "stem": "What do we learn from the study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics?", "options": ["A) Over half of the students interviewed were unaware they were cheating.", "B) Cheating was becoming a way of life for a majority of high school teens.", "C) More than half of the interviewees felt no sense of guilt over cheating.", "D) Cheating was getting more and more difficult for high school students."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】What do we learn from the study 是对某项具体研究内容的客观转述提问,不涉及推断、主\n旨、态度,属于针对单一信息源的细节题。\n【拆句】结构是 What do we learn from the study by the Josephson Institute of Ethics?——主\n干是 what do we learn from + 名词短语,介词短语 by the Josephson Institute of Ethics\n修饰 the study,语序简单、无陷阱。\n【定位】Of the teens surveyed, 51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated at some point on an\nexam but that they did not feel uneasy about the behavior.\n在受访的青少年中,51% 的人说他们曾在某次考试中明知故犯地作弊,但对这种行为并不感到\n不安。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nOver half of the students interviewed were unaware they were cheating. 说\"过半\n受访者不知道自己在作弊\",但定位句明明用了 knowingly,与之相反;\"不知情作弊\"是后文\nCommon Sense Media 部分讨论的内容,被偷换到本题。\nB) 过度推断\nCheating was becoming a way of life for a majority of high school teens. 把\"曾在\n某次考试作弊\"升级为\"成为生活方式\",程度被无端拔高;定位句只说\"曾经作弊过\",并未\n说\"已成为日常\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\n51 percent said that they had knowingly cheated ↔ More than half of the interviewees\ndid not feel uneasy about the behavior ↔ felt no sense of guilt over cheating\nMore than half of the interviewees felt no sense of guilt over cheating. 把\"过\n半\"和\"无负罪感\"同时对上定位句,正是细节题最干净的同义替换。\nD) 与原文相反\nCheating was getting more and more difficult for high school students. 说作弊\n变得越来越难,但全文核心论点是 technology has made it easier than ever,方向完全\n相反。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-47", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 47, "stem": "What did the Common Sense Media survey reveal?", "options": ["A) Most parents tended to overprotect their children.", "B) Many students committed cheating unknowingly.", "C) Students were in urgent need of ethical education.", "D) Parents and kids had conflicting ideas over cheating."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】What did the Common Sense Media survey reveal? 同样指向某项具体调查的客观发现,\n属于客观转述,不涉态度/推断。\n【拆句】What did the Common Sense Media survey reveal?——主谓宾极简单,reveal 在此当及\n物动词\"揭示\",一个主语一个宾语,无修饰、无从句。\n【定位】A Common Sense Media survey found that 35 percent of students had cheated via\nsmartphone, though the parents surveyed in that particular study did not believe their kids had\never cheated. In many cases, students did not realize that strategies like looking up answers\non a smartphone were actually cheating at all.\nCommon Sense Media 的一项调查发现 35% 的学生曾用智能手机作弊,尽管该项调查中受\n访的家长并不相信自己的孩子曾经作弊。很多情况下,学生根本没意识到用手机查答案这种做\n法其实就是作弊。\n【选项】A) 无中生有\nMost parents tended to overprotect their children. 中 overprotect(过度保护)是原\n文里完全没出现的概念,文中只说家长 did not believe their kids had ever cheated(不\n相信孩子作过弊),这是\"不知情\"而非\"过度保护\"。\nB) ✓ 正确\nstudents did not realize that strategies like looking up answers on a smartphone were actually\ncheating at all\n↔ Many students committed cheating unknowingly\nlooking up answers on a smartphone were actually cheating ↔ committed cheating\nMany students committed cheating unknowingly. 同时覆盖\"作弊\"和\"不自知\"两个要\n点,正是定位句最干净的同义替换。\nC) 答非所问\nStudents were in urgent need of ethical education. 是\"应该\"层面的建议(末段才有类\n似主张),并非该调查所揭示的事实;47 题问的是 reveal,要求是事实,不是该做什么。\nD) 偷换概念\nParents and kids had conflicting ideas over cheating. 把家长不知情与学生不自知放\n在一起描述为\"观念冲突\",但原文里两方都不认为自己/孩子在作弊,实际上是\"双方都没认\n识到\",不是冲突,而是同向盲区。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-48", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 48, "stem": "Why do students rarely get caught cheating nowadays?", "options": ["A) They copy formulas on their palms.", "B) They help each other to cover up their acts.", "C) They keep changing their ways of cheating.", "D) They make use of modern technology."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】Why do students rarely get caught cheating nowadays? 用 Why 问原因,但原因往往在\n原文同一句或紧邻句给出,不需多步推理,仍属细节定位。\n【拆句】Why do students rarely get caught cheating nowadays?——副词 rarely 是隐性否\n定\"很少/几乎不\",get caught doing 是\"被抓到正在做某事\"的固定结构,nowadays 限定时\n间。基础薄弱考生注意:rarely 不是\"罕见\"的名词,而是\"很少\"的副词。\n【定位】In today's classrooms, students who cheat are rarely caught. There are no formulas written on\nthe insides of hands or students looking across the aisle, or whispering answers to their\nclassmates. Today's students use smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers to aid their\ncheating attempts and leave no trace of their crimes.\n在今天的教室里,作弊的学生很少被抓到。没人再在手心写公式、隔着过道偷看,或者向同学\n小声报答案。今天的学生用智能手机、平板,甚至课堂电脑辅助作弊,不留任何痕迹。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThey copy formulas on their palms. 实际上正是原文用 no formulas written on the\ninsides of hands 否定掉的旧做法,选项把这条已经被否定的现象当成\"现在\"还在做,方向\n恰好反了。\nB) 无中生有\nThey help each other to cover up their acts. 中 cover up(掩盖)的描述在原文里完全\n没有;原文只说技术让作弊\"不留痕迹\",并未说学生互相帮忙打掩护。\nC) 偷换概念\nThey keep changing their ways of cheating. 描述\"学生不断改变作弊方式\",但原文\n是\"作弊方式随时代而变\"——主语是\"作弊方式\",不是\"学生\";而且 keep changing 强调\n持续变化,原文只比较了\"以前 vs 现在\"两个时点。\nD) ✓ 正确\nuse smartphones, tablets or even in-class computers ↔ They make use of modern technology\nto aid their cheating attempts ↔ make use of\nThey make use of modern technology. 用 modern technology 一词覆盖原文三种设\n备,且对应了 to aid their cheating attempts 的\"借助\"语义,是干净的概括化替换。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-49", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 49, "stem": "What does the author think schools should do to tackle cheating?", "options": ["A) Bring policies against cheating up to date.", "B) Reform their exam methods constantly.", "C) Take advantage of the latest technologies.", "D) Alert parents to their children's behaviour."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author think schools should do 问的是作者对学校的具体建议,原文末段\n是直接的祈使/must 句,属客观陈述,不是推断。\n【拆句】What does the author think schools should do to tackle cheating?——主句 What\ndoes the author think,后接宾语从句 schools should do,不定式 to tackle cheating 作目\n的状语,逻辑就是\"作者认为学校该做什么来解决作弊\"。\n【定位】Schools must develop anti-cheating policies that include technology and these policies must\nbe updated consistently.\n学校必须制定包含科技因素的反作弊政策,并且这些政策必须不断更新。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nthese policies must be updated consistently ↔ Bring policies against cheating up to date\nanti-cheating policies ↔ policies against cheating\nBring policies against cheating up to date. 把\"持续更新政策\"这一并列要点提炼\n出来,正命中原文 must be updated consistently;且 against cheating 等值替换\nanti-cheating。\nB) 无中生有\nReform their exam methods constantly. 提到\"改革考试方式\",但原文末段对学校的建\n议只涉及\"制定/更新政策\",未谈考试方式的改革。\nC) 与原文相反\nTake advantage of the latest technologies. 让学校利用最新技术(去解决作弊),但原文\n里技术是问题源——students learn that answers exist conveniently at their\nfingertips through search engines——学校要做的是把技术纳入政策约束,而非利用技\n术解决问题。\nD) 张冠李戴\nAlert parents to their children's behaviour. 让学校去提醒家长,但原文末段对学\n校只字未提家长这件事;\"alert\"一词原文里出现在家长对孩子的位置 alert them to\nunethical behaviours。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-50", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 50, "stem": "What does the author suggest teachers do in the classroom?", "options": ["A) Prevent students from overusing electronic devices.", "B) Develop more effective anti-cheating strategies.", "C) Find more ways to curb students' unethical acts.", "D) Guard against students' misuse of technology."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author suggest teachers do in the classroom? 仍是对原文一句建议句的\n转述,无推断需求,属细节题。\n【拆句】What does the author suggest teachers do in the classroom?——suggest 后接省略\nthat 的宾语从句 teachers (should) do,基础薄弱考生注意:suggest 后从句的 should 常省\n略,do 是动词原形而不是名词。\n【定位】Teachers must stay on guard when it comes to what their students are doing in classrooms and\nhow technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process.\n老师必须时刻警惕——既要警惕学生在课堂里都在做什么,也要警惕科技可能在学习过程中\n扮演的负面角色。\n【选项】A) 偷换概念\nPrevent students from overusing electronic devices. 把\"科技扮演负面角色\"窄化为\n单一的\"过度使用电子设备\";原文要警惕的核心不是\"用得太多\",而是\"用得不当\"(作弊)。\nB) 张冠李戴\nDevelop more effective anti-cheating strategies. 让老师去\"制定更有效的反作弊策\n略\",但末段把\"develop policies\"的任务给了 schools,老师只是\"stay on guard\"。\nC) 过度推断\nFind more ways to curb students' unethical acts. 让老师\"找出更多办法去遏制不道德\n行为\",但原文里老师的动作只是\"警惕\",并未要求老师去找新办法或主动出击。\nD) ✓ 正确\nTeachers must stay on guard ↔ Guard against\nhow technology could be playing a negative role in the learning process ↔\nstudents' misuse of technology\nGuard against students' misuse of technology. 把\"警惕\"与\"科技的负面作用\"两块拼\n成一个干净的动宾结构,正命中原文要点。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-51", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 51, "stem": "What does the author say companies should do in the context of remote work?", "options": ["A) Reform performance evaluation.", "B) Rethink the organizational fundamentals.", "C) Reexamine its effects on employees' behaviors.", "D) Reflect on its differences from working in the office."], "answer": "A", "analysis": "【答案】A （细节题）\n【判型】What does the author say companies should do 在问作者具体让公司做什么，落点是文\n中明示的一条做法，没有 infer / imply / mainly about 之类的字眼，因此走常规定位路径\n即可。\n【拆句】题干主干是 What does the author say companies should do，what 既是 say 的宾语从\n句中 do 的宾语，也是整句的疑问焦点；句尾 in the context of remote work 是介词短语\n状语，限定背景。结构平铺直叙，无陷阱。\n【定位】Organizations should reconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation\nprocedures in light of the shift to remote work.\n组织应当鉴于向远程办公的转变，重新审视其绩效评估程序是否仍然适当。\n【选项】A) ✓ 正确\nreconsider the appropriateness of their performance evaluation procedures ↔\nReform performance evaluation\nReform performance evaluation 既保留了 \"重新审视\"（reform）的动作，也保留了\n\"绩效评估\"（performance evaluation）的对象，完全覆盖预判语义。\nB) 偷换概念\nRethink the organizational fundamentals 把原文的 \"绩效评估制度\" 偷换成 \"组织根\n本\"；原文虽提 a fundamental rethinking of what organizations expect from\nemployees，但 fundamental 在原文是修饰 rethinking 的副词性形容词，并非要 \"重\n审组织根本\" 这一对象。\nC) 无中生有\nReexamine its effects on employees' behaviors 谈的是 \"重新审视远程办公对员工行\n为的影响\"，原文从未谈论 \"对员工行为的影响\" 这一对象，只谈对评估制度的影响。\nD) 答非所问\nReflect on its differences from working in the office 说的是 \"反思远程办公与在办公\n室办公的差异\"，仅对应原文铺垫句 Remote work is about more than just working\nfrom home—it means working differently，没有触及作者真正提出的 \"重审绩效评\n估\" 这一行动建议。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-52", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 52, "stem": "What should be prioritized in assessing employees' remote work?", "options": ["A) The quantity of their output.", "B) The length of their work time.", "C) The quality of their contribution.", "D) The flexibility of their work schedules."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What should be prioritized 问 \"什么应被优先考虑\"，问的是一个具体的优先项；无\n推断信号，原文必有明示。\n【拆句】题干为 What should be prioritized in assessing employees' remote work：被动语态\nshould be prioritized，主语 what 是被优先项；in V-ing 短语作状语，标明范围。结构直\n白，无陷阱。\n【定位】It is likely that the \"first to arrive and last to leave\" mentality is no longer relevant, but should\nbe replaced by a regard for the quality of an employee's contribution to the organization.\n\"早到晚走\" 的心态很可能已不再适用，应该被对员工对组织所作贡献的质量的看重所取代。\n【选项】A) 与原文相反\nThe quantity of their output 关心的是 \"产出的数量\"，与原文 the quality of an\nemployee's contribution 中的 quality 形成数 vs 质的直接对立；第三段 Focusing on\noutput alone can have unintended consequences 也明确反对单看产出。\nB) 与原文相反\nThe length of their work time 即 \"工作时长\"，恰恰是原文 the \"first to arrive and\nlast to leave\" mentality 所代表的旧心态，被作者明确判为 \"no longer relevant\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nthe quality of an employee's contribution to the organization ↔\nThe quality of their contribution\nThe quality of their contribution 把 but 之后的新标准短语几乎原样搬过来，完全等于\n预判结果。\nD) 张冠李戴\nThe flexibility of their work schedules 谈 \"工作时间安排的弹性\"，对应的是第六段\nflexible work schedules，那里讨论的是 \"补偿\" 而非 \"评估优先项\"，被嫁接到了第\n52 题。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-53", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 53, "stem": "What quality in the employees would be of great importance in a remote work context?", "options": ["A) The ability to produce quality work.", "B) The ability to maximize work efficiency.", "C) The ability to finish tasks in a timely manner.", "D) The ability to work with the least supervision."], "answer": "D", "analysis": "【答案】D （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What quality ... would be of great importance 问的是一项被作者点名为重要的素\n质，原文必有明示项。\n【拆句】题干主干 What quality in the employees would be of great importance in a remote\nwork context：what 修饰 quality，构成疑问焦点；in the employees 与 in a remote\nwork context 都是 in 短语作定语 / 状语；would be 是表 \"一般陈述\" 的虚拟语气。无\n陷阱。\n【定位】Qualities like flexibility and the ability to work under minimal supervision might\nbecome critical.\n诸如灵活性以及在极少督导下工作的能力之类的素质可能会变得至关重要。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nThe ability to produce quality work 谈 \"产出高质量工作\"，回到的是第二段 the\nquality of the work，与第 52 题答案重合；第三段开头 Focusing on output alone\ncan have unintended consequences 已明确不再单看产出。\nB) 无中生有\nThe ability to maximize work efficiency 提出 \"工作效率最大化\"，原文从未出现\nefficiency 或与之对应的概念；远程办公段也未谈效率优化。\nC) 张冠李戴\nThe ability to finish tasks in a timely manner 谈 \"按时完成任务\"，把第四段的 timely\nfeedback 嫁接到第三段；原文谈的是反馈要及时，并非要求员工按时交付。\nD) ✓ 正确\nthe ability to work under minimal supervision ↔\nThe ability to work with the least supervision\nThe ability to work with the least supervision 与预判 \"在极少督导下工作的能力\" 在\n动词、对象、形容词强度上一一对应。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-54", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 54, "stem": "Why is it important for firms to provide timely feedback during the pandemic?", "options": ["A) To evaluate their employees of similar rank in a consistent way.", "B) To keep a watchful eye on those employees who perform poorly.", "C) To help employees in need adapt to the new work environment.", "D) To maintain connections with their employees away from office."], "answer": "C", "analysis": "【答案】C （细节题）\n【判型】题干 Why is it important for firms to provide timely feedback 问 \"为何及时反馈很重\n要\"，原文必然存在与之对应的 \"to do / so that / in order to\" 类目的句。\n【拆句】题干 Why is it important for firms to provide timely feedback during the pandemic：\n句型 \"why is it important for sb to do sth\"，it 是形式主语、to provide 是真正主语；\nduring the pandemic 介词短语作状语限定时间。无陷阱。\n【定位】This allows managers to both keep an eye on struggling employees and provide ongoing\nfeedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment.\n这样做既能让管理者关注陷入困境的员工，又能持续就员工如何适应新的工作环境提供\n反馈。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nTo evaluate their employees of similar rank in a consistent way 谈 \"以一致方式评\n估同级员工\"，对应的是第二段 in a way that is consistent for employees of similar\nrank，那里讲的是绩效度量、不是反馈目的。\nB) 以偏概全\nTo keep a watchful eye on those employees who perform poorly 只对应原文 keep\nan eye on struggling employees 这一半目的，遗漏了 provide ongoing feedback\non how employees are adapting；而且 perform poorly 把中性词 struggling 偷换成\n了带负面评价的 \"表现差劲\"。\nC) ✓ 正确\nprovide ongoing feedback on how employees are adapting to their new work environment ↔\nTo help employees in need adapt to the new work environment\nkeep an eye on struggling employees ↔ employees in need\nTo help employees in need adapt to the new work environment 同时命中原文目的\n中的两个核心元素——struggling employees（in need）与 adapting to their new\nwork environment（adapt to the new work environment）。\nD) 无中生有\nTo maintain connections with their employees away from office 谈 \"维持与异地员\n工的联系\"，原文从未把 timely feedback 的目的描述为 \"维持联系\"，连 connection 一\n词都没出现。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}, {"id": "2022-09-1-r-55", "type": "reading", "typeZh": "仔细阅读", "qno": 55, "stem": "What is the author's suggestion to employers who experience the financial impact of the pandemic?", "options": ["A) Urging their employees to adapt to the new situation.", "B) Rewarding their employees in unconventional ways.", "C) Identifying employees who make little contribution.", "D) Allowing their employees to look for part-time jobs."], "answer": "B", "analysis": "【答案】B （细节题）\n【判型】题干 What is the author's suggestion 问一条明示的建议，落点具体，无推断信号词。\n【拆句】题干 What is the author's suggestion to employers who experience the financial\nimpact of the pandemic：主干 \"What is the author's suggestion\"，to employers 是\nsuggestion 的对象状语，who 引导定语从句修饰 employers。结构略长但逻辑清晰，无\n陷阱。\n【定位】For organizations that have struggled to keep the lights on due to the pandemic, this might\nmean thinking of non-financial ways to reward employees, like unpaid time off or flexible\nwork schedules.\n对于那些因疫情挣扎着维持运营的组织而言，这或许意味着要考虑用非财务的方式来奖励员\n工，比如无薪假期或弹性工作安排。\n【选项】A) 张冠李戴\nUrging their employees to adapt to the new situation 把建议的发出对象与动作都改\n了：原文要求雇主 \"改用非金钱奖励\"，而本项变成 \"催促员工适应新形势\"。adapt to\nthe new situation 又把 adapting to their new work environment 拼贴过来。\nB) ✓ 正确\nnon-financial ways to reward employees ↔\nRewarding their employees in unconventional ways\nRewarding their employees in unconventional ways 既保留了原文的核心动作\nreward，又把 non-financial 抽象到 unconventional，与预判 \"用非金钱 / 非常规方式\n奖励员工\" 完全合拍。\nC) 无中生有\nIdentifying employees who make little contribution 谈 \"识别低贡献员工\"，原文从\n未提出在疫情下要 \"挑出低贡献员工\" 这种建议；contribution 一词只在第二段衡量标准\n中出现，与第六段建议无关。\nD) 过度推断\nAllowing their employees to look for part-time jobs 谈 \"允许员工找兼职\"，原文虽\n提到 unpaid time off or flexible work schedules，但没有任何 \"找兼职\" 的建议；二\n者不可等同。", "points": ["仔细阅读", "细节题"]}]});
